window.EXAM_DATA = {"questions":[{"id":"question-300","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A retailer uses a Dialogflow CX flow through a flow-based agent in CX Agent Studio. An order lookup has populated the session parameter item_category. On the current page, electronics must enter the warranty flow and other recognized categories must enter the refund flow. Which configuration implements this decision?","answers":{"A":"Attach no-match handlers to the current page, one for each item category.","B":"Add mutually exclusive condition routes that inspect item_category and target the appropriate flow.","C":"Add two routes with the same return-order intent and rely on the model to select the correct target.","D":"Place the category rules in the page's entry response so fulfillment selects the next flow."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Condition routes evaluate session state and can transition to another flow. No-match handlers address unmatched input, while an entry response does not establish branching logic. Identical intent requirements do not encode the category distinction. Pages and routes here belong to the reused Dialogflow CX flow. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/handler"},{"id":"question-301","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An HR assistant in Gemini Enterprise Agent Designer retrieves the correct benefits policy, but its answers alternate between long essays and incomplete bullet lists. The team wants every answer to include eligibility, the next action, and a source reference. Which change most directly addresses the problem without code?","answers":{"A":"Specify the required response sections in the instructions and provide several representative input-output examples.","B":"Increase the number of retrieved policy passages and let the model choose a format based on their structure.","C":"Restrict retrieval to documents with matching section headings, removing policies that use different layouts.","D":"Add a response-length limit and ask for concise answers, leaving the content and structure implicit."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Clear output requirements and examples guide formatting and coverage. Retrieval changes address available evidence, which is already correct here, and a length limit alone does not specify required fields. These instructions improve consistency but still require testing; they do not provide deterministic control of hidden reasoning. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/prompts/prompt-design-strategies"},{"id":"question-302","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A firm imports proprietary policy PDFs from Cloud Storage into Gemini Enterprise. Employees authenticate through the organization's configured identity provider, and each PDF has a different set of permitted readers. Which import configuration is necessary to preserve document-level access in search?","answers":{"A":"Grant the ingestion service account access to the bucket, and use the bucket's IAM policy as the search-result filter.","B":"Encrypt the documents with a customer-managed key, and grant every employee access to the search application.","C":"Create an access-controlled data store and import document ACL metadata with reader identities that match the configured identity system.","D":"Include permitted department names in ordinary document metadata and instruct the assistant to suppress results for other departments."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Custom Cloud Storage imports require an access-controlled data store and ACL metadata identifying readers. The identity provider supplies the searching user's identity. Ingestion IAM grants, encryption, and ordinary metadata plus prompt instructions do not substitute for document-level retrieval authorization. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/identity"},{"id":"question-303","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A knowledge assistant uses a Gemini Enterprise unstructured-document data store populated from Cloud Storage. The team now needs answers about what was said in archived MP3 training recordings. The selected import path accepts document formats rather than raw audio. What should the team do?","answers":{"A":"Create document records containing only recording titles, durations, and MP3 URLs, then enable semantic search.","B":"Transcribe the recordings with a supported service and ingest the transcripts as supported documents with source and time references.","C":"Upload the recordings to the same location and enable the PDF layout parser to extract their spoken content.","D":"Change each recording's MIME type to text/plain in the import manifest so the existing parser indexes its contents."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The chosen document importer needs a searchable representation of speech. Transcription produces that representation; recording identifiers and timestamps help users locate the evidence. Metadata alone omits the spoken material, and neither a document parser nor a MIME-type change converts audio into text. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/capabilities/audio-understanding; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/prepare-data"},{"id":"question-304","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"During required order-number collection in a Dialogflow CX flow reused by CX Agent Studio, the caller may remain silent. The first two no-input attempts should re-prompt; the third should enter the configured escalation page. Which configuration provides this behavior?","answers":{"A":"Use three no-match handlers on the form parameter, with the third targeting the escalation page.","B":"Configure the parameter's numbered no-input reprompt handlers, with escalation on the third event.","C":"Place an escalation instruction in the page description and let generative fallback count silent turns.","D":"Set a single no-input handler to re-enter the page and put the escalation response in its entry fulfillment."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Numbered no-input handlers support successive silence responses, including a transition on the third occurrence. Parameter reprompt handlers are the appropriate scope during form filling. No-match events concern invalid input; re-entering the page or describing retry behavior does not define the required sequence. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/handler"},{"id":"question-305","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity coding agent must consult current Jira issues and query an internal API catalog while planning changes. Both systems expose authenticated APIs, and the team wants reusable, callable integrations. What should the architect configure?","answers":{"A":"A scheduled export of both systems into workspace Markdown files, with instructions to treat the latest export as authoritative.","B":"Vetted MCP servers exposing the required queries, with authentication and tool access scoped to the team's needs.","C":"A workspace skill containing endpoint descriptions and sample responses, without providing API execution tools.","D":"A custom model trained on issue history and API specifications, refreshed whenever the catalog changes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"MCP provides callable access to live external systems. Exports and training snapshots become stale; instructions alone do not implement authenticated API calls. Server configuration and authorization are separate requirements. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/"},{"id":"question-306","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A platform will run coding agents that execute integration tests and arbitrary build scripts on GKE. Tests use synthetic data; production database access is unnecessary. Which design best reduces host compromise, production access, and unauthorized data transfer?","answers":{"A":"Use separate Kubernetes namespaces and unrestricted outbound networking, with a shared developer credential for dependency installation.","B":"Use GKE Sandbox, scoped test identities, restricted mounts, and enforced default-deny networking with approved egress destinations.","C":"Use regular privileged Pods with read-only production credentials, and record every shell command for subsequent security review.","D":"Use GKE Sandbox with the developer's home directory mounted and allow internet access so builds do not need network exceptions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"GKE Sandbox adds a host-isolation layer, while identities, mounts, and network policies limit reachable assets. Namespaces alone are insufficient; privileged Pods and broad credentials enlarge exposure. Sandboxing does not automatically protect accessible external services or prevent every exfiltration path. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/sandbox-pods"},{"id":"question-307","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A coding agent is assigned confirmed SQL-injection findings and a vulnerable dependency in a Rails application. The security team requires evidence that the issues are fixed and normal behavior is preserved. Which workflow meets that requirement?","answers":{"A":"Reproduce the findings, patch them on a branch, rerun targeted security checks and regression tests in isolation, and submit the diff and results for review.","B":"Upgrade the dependency and regenerate the tests from the modified implementation, then submit the change when the regenerated suite passes.","C":"Patch each location named by the scanner and submit the scanner's original report as evidence, leaving functional verification to production monitoring.","D":"Place an input filter in front of the application and suppress the findings until a later release, while retaining the vulnerable implementation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Remediation needs evidence for both the vulnerability and preserved behavior. Tests derived only from changed code can encode mistakes; old scan results do not validate a fix; compensating filters and suppression leave the stated remediation incomplete. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/overview"},{"id":"question-308","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity team needs its commit-message convention available on every task, but its lengthy database-migration procedure only when relevant. Both must be maintained with the repository. Which setup fits?","answers":{"A":"Place both documents in one manually activated workspace rule and ask each developer to remember to enable it before every task.","B":"Use an Always On workspace rule for the convention and a focused workspace skill with a clear migration-task description.","C":"Put the convention and complete migration procedure in a post-edit hook that injects both documents after every file change.","D":"Create global skills on the lead developer's machine and rely on other contributors' agents to discover them through Git."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Rule activation can be explicitly Always On; skills load detailed procedures when relevant. Manual activation does not satisfy every-task availability, repeated hook injection wastes context, and one user's global files are not repository-shared. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/rules-workflows/"},{"id":"question-309","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A monorepo change has independent documentation and load-test work alongside a payments refactor. All streams have agreed interfaces. How should an architect use Antigravity subagents to gain parallelism while controlling integration risk?","answers":{"A":"Run all agents in the same checkout with overlapping file ownership, then accept the most recent version of each conflicting file.","B":"Delegate bounded streams with explicit interfaces and separate worktrees where needed; have the parent integrate results and run cross-module tests.","C":"Give each agent the whole task and ask it to produce a complete alternative implementation, then merge all implementations together.","D":"Run the streams sequentially in one conversation and retain every search result so the final agent has the broadest possible history."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Scoped delegation enables parallel work; worktrees and explicit ownership reduce editing conflicts. Shared edits need coordination, complete competing implementations duplicate effort, and serial execution forfeits available parallelism. Integration tests remain necessary after combining independently completed work. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/subagents/"},{"id":"question-310","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team already uses Antigravity to build an ADK agent. It wants reusable guidance and commands for scaffolding, evaluation, Google Cloud deployment, and operational observability. Which addition is designed for that lifecycle?","answers":{"A":"Gemini CLI, used as a replacement coding assistant with the same repository context and existing application prompts.","B":"Google Agents CLI, installed so Antigravity can use its agent-development skills and lifecycle commands.","C":"Cloud Build, configured to compile the repository and deploy a container whenever the main branch changes.","D":"An MCP server exposing project files, with the current deployment runbook returned as a searchable resource."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agents CLI equips coding assistants with specialized development and deployment workflows. Gemini CLI is a coding assistant, Cloud Build handles pipeline execution, and a documentation MCP server supplies context; none alone supplies the described Agents CLI lifecycle toolchain. Reference: https://github.com/google/agents-cli"},{"id":"question-311","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An organization approves Claude Code but requires model requests to use its Google Cloud project and IAM-controlled access to Claude models. Repository shell and MCP permissions must remain separately restricted. Which architecture fits?","answers":{"A":"Configure Claude Code's Vertex backend for the approved project and model access, authenticate with Google credentials, and separately scope local and MCP tools.","B":"Configure a project-scoped MCP server named Vertex AI, while leaving Claude Code authenticated to a developer's personal Anthropic account.","C":"Run Claude Code on a Compute Engine VM using its default personal subscription; the VM's project automatically becomes the model billing project.","D":"Grant the developer a Vertex AI role and approve every Claude Code tool call, while retaining the existing personal model-provider configuration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Vertex backend and Google authentication determine the model-access route. Hosting the client on Google Cloud or adding MCP does not select that backend. Cloud IAM for model invocation also does not replace local tool permission controls. Reference: https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/announcing-claude-apps-gateway-for-google-cloud"},{"id":"question-312","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A support email classifier handles two million messages monthly. In representative tests, an SLM and a larger LLM both meet its accuracy target, but the SLM is cheaper and faster. Only the larger LLM meets a separate research agent's synthesis-quality target. Which deployment strategy best fits these results?","answers":{"A":"Use the larger LLM for both workloads to standardize model operations.","B":"Use the SLM for classification and the larger LLM for research.","C":"Use the SLM for both workloads and increase the research prompt length.","D":"Run both models on every message and always ask the larger model to arbitrate disagreements."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Select models using measured task quality, latency, and cost. The tested SLM meets the classifier's requirements, while the larger model is justified for research. Standardizing on one size or invoking both unnecessarily ignores those results; a longer prompt does not establish that the smaller model meets the research target. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/model-garden/explore-models"},{"id":"question-313","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"One team requires licensed access to modify model weights and must run inference on infrastructure it controls inside its VPC. Another has no serving-operations team and accepts a provider-hosted, usage-billed API. Which pairing best matches these requirements?","answers":{"A":"A region-selected proprietary SaaS model for the first team; a self-managed model cluster for the second.","B":"A suitably licensed self-hosted open-weight model for the first team; a managed model API for the second.","C":"Managed open-model APIs for both teams, because an open license makes the provider's serving infrastructure customer-controlled.","D":"Self-hosted models for both teams, with provider billing covering infrastructure operations."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Weight access and infrastructure control favor self-hosting under an appropriate license. A managed API reduces serving operations for the other team. Regional SaaS access does not satisfy the stated infrastructure requirement, and an open-weight license does not dictate hosting. Managed offerings can support tuning, so tuning alone does not exclude SaaS. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/model-garden/explore-models"},{"id":"question-314","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A Python ADK agent needs to choose between a Rails order-status API and a parameterized BigQuery reporting query. The tools must validate arguments and use narrowly scoped credentials. Which integration design fits?","answers":{"A":"Register typed function tools or suitable toolsets that implement the API and query operations.","B":"Run both operations before every model turn and append all results to the conversation history.","C":"Expose one unrestricted HTTP-and-SQL executor and describe permitted use in the system instruction.","D":"Ask the model to generate an executable Python fragment containing credentials and both client calls."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"ADK exposes functions and toolsets to the model through documented interfaces. The implementation remains responsible for validation, authorization, and execution. Eagerly running every operation wastes work; an unrestricted executor increases authority; generated code containing credentials is an inappropriate tool boundary. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-315","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A travel agent must retain each booking conversation's progress and automatically extract useful preferences from completed conversations for relevant recall weeks later. The team wants managed services rather than its own memory-extraction pipeline. Which combination fits?","answers":{"A":"Managed sessions, with every previous transcript concatenated into each new prompt.","B":"Managed sessions plus user-scoped Agent Platform Memory Bank generation and retrieval.","C":"Memory Bank alone, using extracted memories as the authoritative state of an unfinished booking.","D":"A shared application-state dictionary, with all travelers' preferences loaded into each invocation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Sessions maintain conversational progress; Memory Bank generates and retrieves longer-term information when integrated into the application. Extracted memories are not an authoritative booking ledger. Replaying all transcripts is costly, and shared application state would mix users. Cross-session preferences can also use persistent user state, but that does not provide the requested extraction capability. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/memory-bank"},{"id":"question-316","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team wants both coding-agent-assisted and direct terminal workflows for building ADK agents with Agents CLI. Separately, its deployed finance agent must require approval before posting a journal entry. Which interpretation correctly separates the development interface from runtime approval?","answers":{"A":"Use CLI skills with a coding agent or commands directly; implement runtime approvals separately.","B":"Agent mode approves all deployed tool calls; human mode pauses before every deployed tool call.","C":"Human mode creates only prompts, whereas agent mode is required to produce deployable ADK code.","D":"The modes select whether Agent Runtime authenticates as a workload principal or as an end user."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Agents CLI supports coding-agent-assisted development and direct terminal use. These development interfaces do not establish per-action runtime approval or choose runtime identity. Both can build deployable agents; consequential tool operations need an explicit confirmation and authorization implementation. Reference: https://google.github.io/agents-cli/guide/getting-started/"},{"id":"question-317","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A RAG evaluation finds the supporting passage among the top 50 candidates for 95% of queries, but it rarely reaches the five passages supplied to the generator. The candidate-generation latency is acceptable. Which change most directly targets this failure?","answers":{"A":"Increase the generator's parameter count while keeping the same five input passages.","B":"Rerank the 50 candidates for relevance before selecting the final five.","C":"Increase chunk overlap throughout the corpus and retain the existing candidate ordering.","D":"Raise the minimum first-stage similarity score so fewer candidates reach selection."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The evidence is already in the candidate pool, making final ordering the immediate bottleneck. Reranking can improve the selected context. A larger generator cannot use evidence it never receives; chunking changes target a different stage; a stricter first-stage threshold can remove useful candidates. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/retrieval-and-ranking"},{"id":"question-318","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team wants managed document ingestion, transformation, embedding, and retrieval components for a RAG application. Another already owns those components and needs an ANN index service whose index configuration it controls. Which mapping best matches these requirements?","answers":{"A":"RAG Engine for the first team; Vector Search 1.0 for the second.","B":"Vector Search 1.0 for the first team; RAG Engine for the second.","C":"Agent Registry for the first team; Agent Search for the second.","D":"Memory Bank for the first team; an Agent Runtime session for the second."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"RAG Engine supplies managed RAG components, while Vector Search 1.0 supplies an index for a custom retrieval pipeline. Neither registration nor conversational memory replaces document retrieval. Databases can also support vector search, but their capabilities do not make these service roles interchangeable. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/rag-overview ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/configuring-indexes"},{"id":"question-319","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A procurement agent uses its own Agent Identity for direct Google Cloud requests. Its allowed resources are in a procurement project, and all relevant operations support principal access boundary enforcement. How should its access be configured?","answers":{"A":"Grant broad organization roles and attach procurement resource labels for audit reporting.","B":"Grant the needed roles and bind a PAB restricting eligible resources to the procurement project.","C":"Bind a PAB to the procurement project and omit role grants, since the boundary grants access.","D":"Keep a shared administrator credential in the tool and apply the PAB only to the calling agent."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Allow policies grant permissions; a PAB limits resource eligibility for permissions it enforces. A PAB does not grant access or automatically constrain a tool using a different principal. Broad grants plus labels are not equivalent to an enforced resource boundary. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/principal-access-boundary-policies"},{"id":"question-320","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK team needs supported Google Cloud data tools, a custom integration with a SaaS CRM, and a catalog where other teams can discover those capabilities. Which architecture assigns the service responsibilities correctly?","answers":{"A":"Use Agent Registry to execute all database and SaaS API calls, with Cloud DNS storing tool schemas.","B":"Use Google Cloud MCP servers where supported, a custom SaaS MCP server, and Agent Registry for discovery.","C":"Use Agent Search connectors as write-capable tools and Memory Bank as the organization-wide capability catalog.","D":"Use A2A Agent Cards as database connectors and deploy every third-party API inside Agent Registry."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Managed MCP servers expose supported Google Cloud capabilities; unsupported SaaS operations can use custom MCP integrations. Agent Registry catalogs these endpoints and tools. It is not their execution host, and search connectors or A2A capability descriptions do not implement arbitrary database or SaaS operations. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/overview ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/agent-registry/overview"},{"id":"question-321","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK agent delegates an investigation to a partner's independently implemented agent, which manages its own reasoning and task progress. Each agent also invokes database and API capabilities. Which protocol assignment best matches these interface roles?","answers":{"A":"A2A for delegated agent tasks; MCP for exposed tools and contextual resources.","B":"MCP resources for delegated agent tasks; A2A task artifacts for database invocation.","C":"A2A for all interfaces, because it replaces MCP when more than one agent is involved.","D":"MCP for all interfaces, because every remote agent must disclose its internal tools and prompts."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A2A supports cooperation with agents that retain their implementation autonomy. MCP exposes tools and context through standardized interfaces. They are complementary: neither requires replacing the other or revealing a remote agent's internal reasoning and implementation. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/ ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/overview"},{"id":"question-322","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"In an existing ADK template workflow, order validation must finish before inventory and fraud checks. Those two checks are independent, and the decision step needs both results. Which composition preserves the dependencies while permitting concurrency?","answers":{"A":"A ParallelAgent containing validation, both checks, and the final decision agent.","B":"A SequentialAgent containing validation, a ParallelAgent for the checks, and a merger/decision step.","C":"A LoopAgent containing the checks, with validation performed after the loop completes.","D":"An LLM coordinator instructed to prefer the order, with all four agents available for optional delegation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A sequential outer workflow establishes dependency order; an inner parallel workflow runs independent checks before the merger. Parallelizing every stage races the decision against its inputs. A loop has different semantics, and optional model-selected delegation does not enforce the required dependency structure. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/workflow-agents/sequential-agents/ ; https://adk.dev/agents/workflow-agents/parallel-agents/"},{"id":"question-323","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A support agent on Agent Runtime may delegate only to an approved redaction agent before analytics receives a minimized result. The team registers all endpoints, but the support agent can still call analytics directly. Which change closes the missing enforcement path?","answers":{"A":"Add a forbidden-handoff label to the analytics Agent Registry entry and retain direct network access.","B":"Route relevant calls through Agent Gateway with identity-based policies and prevent bypass of that path.","C":"Enable TLS for both endpoints and use the same service credential for every caller.","D":"Instruct the analytics agent to ignore PII after receiving the complete support transcript."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Registration provides inventory; enforcement requires a controlled request path and policies evaluated for the caller. Preventing bypass makes that path meaningful. TLS protects transport but does not decide permitted handoffs, and recipient-side prompt filtering exposes information before it can reject it. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/agent-gateway-runtime-deploy"},{"id":"question-324","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Choose two. A Polish/English knowledge base loses cross-language matches and often splits table headings from the rows needed to answer questions. Which two ingestion or embedding changes directly address those diagnosed problems?","answers":{"A":"Use an embedding model evaluated for cross-language retrieval across both languages.","B":"Increase the generation model's output limit while retaining the existing index.","C":"Use structure-aware chunks that preserve headings and related table content.","D":"Replace semantic retrieval with an exact-match filter on the user's query language."},"correct_answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Cross-language embeddings address language alignment; structure-aware parsing and chunking preserve information relationships. Increasing generated output does not repair missing context, and filtering by query language can exclude the English evidence a Polish question needs. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/embeddings/get-text-embeddings ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/layout-parser-integration"},{"id":"question-325","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A team is preparing a reusable benchmark for a claims agent. It must cover real user intents, required tool actions, valid final answers, and ambiguous cases that previously failed. Which dataset design best supports repeatable regression evaluation?","answers":{"A":"Store recent production prompts with the current agent responses accepted automatically as ground truth.","B":"Version reviewed cases covering real intents and edge cases, with expected actions and outcomes.","C":"Generate many prompt paraphrases and accept them when two runs of the same candidate produce matching answers.","D":"Use all development examples, including those embedded in the system prompt, as the sole release benchmark."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A useful golden dataset records independently reviewed expectations and covers relevant variations. Unreviewed model outputs can preserve existing errors; candidate agreement is not ground truth. Reusing only prompt-development examples gives an overly optimistic estimate of generalization. Reference: https://adk.dev/evaluate/; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluation-dataset"},{"id":"question-326","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A refund agent gives plausible final answers but sometimes calls issue_refund before lookup_order or supplies an incorrect amount. Every test case has one required tool sequence with known arguments. Which evaluation most directly detects these regressions?","answers":{"A":"Compare final responses with reference text using a strict ROUGE threshold.","B":"Apply ADK tool_trajectory_avg_score with EXACT matching to the expected tool calls and arguments.","C":"Judge only whether the final response states that order verification occurred before the refund.","D":"Check that both tool names occur somewhere in a trace, allowing arbitrary arguments and additional calls."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Exact trajectory matching checks tool names, arguments, and order, rejecting extra or missing calls. Text similarity and claims in the final response do not establish what executed. Checking tool presence alone misses incorrect amounts and reversed dependencies. Reference: https://adk.dev/evaluate/criteria/"},{"id":"question-327","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A team needs (i) repeatable ADK regression tests in its local development loop, (ii) centrally managed evaluation of agent quality, and (iii) a reusable domain-specific LLM judging rubric. Which approach fits these needs without assuming the tools are mutually exclusive?","answers":{"A":"Use Cloud Trace for all three, because traces automatically assign semantic quality scores to every agent event.","B":"Use ADK evalsets locally, the Agent Platform Gen AI evaluation service centrally, and a custom autorater rubric integrated into evaluation.","C":"Use ADK evalsets locally and centrally, but replace the agent model with the autorater so it evaluates its own production answers.","D":"Use managed evaluation only for text-overlap metrics, and implement a separate evaluation platform for every custom LLM rubric."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ADK supports the developer evaluation loop. Managed evaluation can centralize assessments and accept custom LLM metrics. A custom autorater supplies task-specific judgment; it need not require an entirely separate platform. Traces provide evidence, while self-grading needs independent validation. Reference: https://adk.dev/evaluate/; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/manage-metrics"},{"id":"question-328","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A prompt change preserves answer quality but causes an agent to bypass a required eligibility lookup. What CI/CD release gate would best prevent this class of regression?","answers":{"A":"Gate promotion on candidate evalset results for both required tool execution and response quality.","B":"Gate deployment on the aggregate final-answer score, then investigate tool behavior only if that score decreases.","C":"Run static checks on tool signatures and imports; accept a candidate when its callable interfaces remain unchanged.","D":"Run a weekly production evaluation and let new versions deploy automatically between scheduled evaluation runs."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A release gate must assess the behavior that can fail, including required tool execution. Interface checks do not verify orchestration, and answer scores can hide skipped steps. Scheduled production evaluation complements the gate but cannot stop an unqualified release. Reference: https://codelabs.developers.google.com/adk-eval/instructions"},{"id":"question-329","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Select runtimes for three workloads. A uses ADK and prioritizes managed agent hosting and session integration with minimal infrastructure operations. B requires custom Kubernetes node pools and pod-level NetworkPolicies. C is a stateless HTTP tool with bursty traffic and a scale-to-zero requirement. Which mapping best fits?","answers":{"A":"A: GKE; B: Agent Runtime; C: Cloud Run.","B":"A: Agent Runtime; B: GKE; C: Cloud Run.","C":"A: Cloud Run; B: Cloud Run; C: GKE.","D":"A: Agent Runtime; B: Cloud Run; C: GKE."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Managed agent hosting favors Agent Runtime, Kubernetes-specific controls favor GKE, and a stateless scale-to-zero HTTP service fits Cloud Run. Cloud Run can host agents and some GPU workloads; the explicit Kubernetes requirements, rather than GPU availability alone, distinguish the second workload. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale; https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/performance-optimization; https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/network-policy"},{"id":"question-330","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An ADK agent repeatedly invokes search with almost identical arguments until its request times out. The tool returns successfully each time. Which intervention best combines diagnosis with immediate containment?","answers":{"A":"Raise the request timeout and retry allowance, then compare the resulting final-answer scores.","B":"Inspect model/tool events and add a bounded LLM-call or iteration limit while correcting the repeated-call logic.","C":"Increase serving replicas and model quota so repeated calls are less likely to time out under load.","D":"Cache search results indefinitely so the repeated tool calls finish quickly enough to return a final answer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Repeated successful calls indicate an orchestration loop rather than inadequate capacity. ADK exposes a maximum LLM-call budget; loop-specific guards can further constrain behavior. Timeouts, replicas, or caching alone do not correct the decision to repeat work. Reference: https://adk.dev/runtime/runconfig/"},{"id":"question-331","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent runs unchanged code and prompts, but quality declines after its source catalog and selected model alias change. Which investigation best separates possible drift from an application regression?","answers":{"A":"Attribute the decline to the latest model and roll back without testing the changed catalog.","B":"Replay representative cases with controlled model/corpus versions and compare production quality slices.","C":"Increase temperature to recover answer diversity, keeping the model and corpus changes combined during evaluation.","D":"Evaluate only infrastructure health because unchanged source code rules out a change in agent behavior."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agent behavior also depends on models, data, and traffic. Controlled comparisons help isolate which changing input contributes to degradation; production monitoring detects distribution changes. A coincident model change is not proof of causation, and healthy infrastructure does not establish answer quality. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/reliability"},{"id":"question-332","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent p95 latency doubles. Traces show stable model time and a slow MCP tool that queries Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL; a child span isolates most of the delay to SQL execution. What should the team investigate next?","answers":{"A":"Compare faster model families, preserving the existing database query and connection configuration.","B":"Inspect slow SQL execution with Query Insights and query plans, then validate a targeted fix.","C":"Raise agent concurrency to issue more copies of the slow query simultaneously and reduce queueing.","D":"Cache each result globally for a day without considering account scope or how quickly the underlying records change."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The observed SQL span localizes the bottleneck. Query-level diagnosis can guide indexing or query changes. A faster model cannot remove the measured SQL delay, extra concurrency can worsen contention, and caching requires appropriate freshness and access scope. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/using-query-insights"},{"id":"question-333","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent monthly bill triples while overall request volume rises only slightly. Which monitoring design best distinguishes extra model usage from tool and hosting costs?","answers":{"A":"Track the project billing total and use the current month total divided by requests as the only diagnostic.","B":"Correlate per-agent model usage, tool activity, and runtime metrics with attributable billing data and release identifiers.","C":"Use input-token totals to allocate every charge, including database calls and idle serving instances, across agents.","D":"Collect full prompts in application logs and ask the agent to calculate the invoice from its conversation history."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Usage telemetry by agent and model identifies operational drivers; billing data supplies actual charge context. A project total cannot localize the change. Token totals omit other services, and an agent lacks authoritative invoice data merely because prompts were logged. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/monitoring; https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/cost-optimization"},{"id":"question-334","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A customer-support agent must update tickets in a third-party SaaS system under each employee's own permissions. The SaaS supports OAuth 2.0, and employees can revoke access individually. Which authentication design best meets the requirement?","answers":{"A":"Use a two-legged OAuth provider with a single application credential for all employees.","B":"Configure a three-legged OAuth provider in Agent Identity auth manager and obtain each employee's consent for the required scopes.","C":"Authenticate employees to the frontend, then use a shared administrator API key for all ticket updates.","D":"Use the agent's Google Cloud access token as the SaaS credential without configuring the SaaS authorization server."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Three-legged OAuth provides user delegation and consent, with auth manager handling the delegated credentials. Application credentials and administrator keys authorize the application, not each employee. A Google Cloud credential does not automatically become a valid credential for an unrelated SaaS API. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/auth-with-3lo-v2"},{"id":"question-335","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent receives IAM roles on resources in several organizations. Security wants a principal access boundary (PAB) to restrict its resource eligibility to approved projects. What must the architect verify before claiming that a particular API operation is blocked outside those projects?","answers":{"A":"That every resource outside the projects has an IAM deny policy administered by the agent's organization.","B":"That the binding includes the agent and the PAB enforcement version covers the operation.","C":"That the agent's project disables creation of service account keys, which activates PAB enforcement for all APIs.","D":"That the destination uses a private IP address, because PAB policies evaluate network routes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"PAB enforcement is principal-based and covers a defined set of IAM permissions determined by the policy's enforcement version. It is not a universal API or network restriction. External resource deny policies are not required for PAB evaluation, and service account key restrictions do not expand its coverage. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/principal-access-boundary-policies"},{"id":"question-336","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"Agents on Agent Runtime call registered MCP servers and other agents. Security needs centrally collected interaction telemetry identifying the participating agents and destinations, without relying on instrumentation added independently by each team. Which design best fits?","answers":{"A":"Collect governed interaction telemetry through Agent Gateway and its observability integrations.","B":"Use Agent Registry inventory alone to infer which registered capabilities were invoked.","C":"Use Cloud NAT connection logs as the sole record of agent identities and individual tool interactions.","D":"Use billing export alone to reconstruct the sequence of agent-to-agent calls."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Agent Gateway provides interaction telemetry at the network layer for traffic it governs. Registry metadata identifies available resources but does not prove invocation. NAT logs and billing records lack the agent-protocol detail required to reconstruct these interactions. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/govern/gateways/agent-gateway-overview"},{"id":"question-337","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A team screens every text prompt and response with Model Armor. An attack spreads instructions across multiple turns and includes a Base64-encoded payload that the agent later decodes. Which conclusion should guide the security review?","answers":{"A":"Add application-level controls for conversation context, decoded content, and tool execution.","B":"Model Armor reconstructs the conversation automatically when each request has the same session identifier.","C":"Raising detection confidence thresholds makes Model Armor decode and inspect every encoded representation.","D":"Screening model responses alone guarantees that a malicious tool action cannot execute beforehand."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Model Armor evaluates requests independently and does not decode encoded content. Application-level controls must address these gaps and constrain actions even when screening misses manipulation. Session identifiers and confidence settings do not add those capabilities, and response screening cannot undo an earlier tool execution. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/overview"},{"id":"question-338","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A payment agent may execute transfers up to EUR 10,000 automatically. Larger transfers require approval by an authorized finance reviewer. Where should the amount rule and approval requirement be enforced?","answers":{"A":"In deterministic tool or workflow logic that pauses execution above the threshold and validates approval before releasing the transfer.","B":"In the system prompt, with the payment tool accepting any transfer the model elects to submit.","C":"In a post-execution monitoring rule that alerts finance and requests retrospective approval.","D":"In a model confidence threshold that automatically substitutes for approval when the model is sufficiently certain."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A deterministic pre-execution gate can require human confirmation based on the transaction amount. The application must also authenticate and authorize the reviewer. Prompt instructions and model confidence are not approval enforcement, while retrospective review happens after funds have moved. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/confirmation/"},{"id":"question-339","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An analytics agent authenticates employees in its web frontend but submits all BigQuery MCP queries using its broadly privileged workload credential. Existing BigQuery controls distinguish employees' entitlements. How should queries be changed to respect the requesting employee's access?","answers":{"A":"Add the authenticated employee's email to the prompt while retaining the workload credential for BigQuery.","B":"Use user-delegated BigQuery OAuth credentials bound to the authenticated employee.","C":"Register the agent in Agent Registry and assume registration changes the effective BigQuery caller.","D":"Use a shared query cache keyed only by SQL text so that each user receives consistent results."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Frontend sign-in alone does not change the credential presented downstream. A user-delegated BigQuery OAuth integration lets the data service authorize the user. An email in a prompt or a registry entry is not delegation, and a cross-user cache can disclose results computed under another user's authority. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/auth-with-3lo-v2"},{"id":"question-340","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A bank must fine-tune a model with accessible weights and serve document triage entirely on its own VPC infrastructure. It targets p99 below 400 ms at projected peak load. No candidate has been load-tested. Which selection approach is defensible?","answers":{"A":"Select a region-hosted managed model; regional placement alone satisfies the VPC requirement.","B":"Benchmark a suitably licensed self-hosted SLM for triage quality, peak-load latency, and total serving cost.","C":"Select a self-hosted SLM and commit to the latency target based solely on its parameter count.","D":"Deploy the largest open model because capacity planning is unnecessary when accuracy improves."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Self-hosting addresses the stated infrastructure and weight-control requirements, but model size does not prove an SLA. Representative quality and load measurements are required. SaaS regional placement is a different boundary, and selecting the largest model without capacity evidence does not resolve latency or cost. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/model-garden/explore-models"},{"id":"question-341","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Choose two. An ADK agent repeatedly sends a large unchanged policy prefix on a supported Gemini model. It also needs to extract and recall durable user preferences across sessions. Which two capabilities address these separate needs?","answers":{"A":"Configure eligible model context caching and measure cache reuse and cost.","B":"Move the policy into a session-state key and continue injecting its full text on every request.","C":"Integrate user-scoped Memory Bank generation and retrieval.","D":"Raise the output-token limit and store every preference in invocation-temporary state."},"correct_answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Context caching can reduce repeated-prefix processing charges when requests meet cache requirements; storing text in state alone is not caching. Memory Bank supplies extracted cross-session memories. Temporary invocation state expires, and output limits do not solve either need. Reference: https://adk.dev/context/caching/ ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/memory-bank"},{"id":"question-342","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"For many RAG queries, the supporting passage is absent from all 50 first-stage candidates. A better reranker barely changes answer quality. Which next experiment targets the diagnosed failure?","answers":{"A":"Increase reranker capacity while retaining the same candidate set.","B":"Evaluate first-stage embedding fit and chunk construction on the missed-query set.","C":"Give the generator a longer context window but send the same 50 candidates.","D":"Reduce the final candidate count from 50 to five before applying the existing reranker."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Candidate recall is the bottleneck: reranking cannot introduce passages absent from its input. Testing embedding suitability and chunk construction addresses candidate generation. A larger context window with unchanged evidence or a smaller candidate pool does not recover the missing passage. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/rag-overview"},{"id":"question-343","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Two deployments use identical unnormalized embeddings but rank documents differently: one uses cosine similarity and the other dot product. The team intends cosine-based relevance. Which explanation and correction fit?","answers":{"A":"The metrics rank vectors identically; rebuild both indexes with additional shards.","B":"Dot product includes magnitude; standardize cosine or normalize query and document vectors.","C":"Cosine requires sparse vectors; convert the corpus to keyword weights and retain dot product.","D":"Only query magnitude matters; normalize query vectors while leaving all indexed document vectors unchanged."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Cosine divides out vector norms, while dot product includes them. Consistent metrics or unit normalization align the intended ranking; normalizing only queries leaves document magnitudes influential. Sharding does not change the metric definition, and cosine is not restricted to sparse representations. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/configuring-indexes"},{"id":"question-344","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A partner's A2A agent is implemented in a different framework. Your client needs its advertised skills, supported interfaces, and authentication requirements before delegating. Which protocol artifact supplies this information?","answers":{"A":"An MCP tools/list response.","B":"The partner's Agent Card.","C":"The partner's ADK session.","D":"A Cloud Trace invocation span."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An Agent Card describes an A2A agent's public interface and capabilities. It can be discovered through a well-known location or a catalog; a registry complements this mechanism. Tool listings, session internals, and traces do not substitute for the A2A contract, and the card itself does not grant access. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/"},{"id":"question-345","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A remote MCP server calls a SaaS ticketing API on each user's behalf. The SaaS enforces user entitlements and records user-attributed actions. How should the server handle access tokens across the two authorization boundaries?","answers":{"A":"Forward the MCP access token unchanged to the SaaS API so the same credential identifies the user at both hops.","B":"Validate the token intended for the MCP server and obtain a separate, appropriately scoped user-delegated SaaS token.","C":"Use an administrator SaaS API key and ask the model to remove unauthorized tickets from the result.","D":"Accept any validly signed user token, regardless of audience, and use its subject as proof of SaaS authorization."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"MCP requires audience-bound validation and prohibits token passthrough. The server acts as an OAuth client to the SaaS, using a separate delegated token for that resource. Administrator credentials plus prompt filtering bypass entitlements; a signed identity claim alone does not establish authorization for another API. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/authorization"},{"id":"question-346","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An existing ADK template-based writing workflow must execute draft then critique repeatedly, stopping when an implemented rubric check passes or after five iterations. Which design implements both repetition and bounded termination?","answers":{"A":"A SequentialAgent that invokes draft and critique once, with no outer control structure.","B":"A LoopAgent with ordered draft and critique sub-agents, max_iterations=5, and an explicit early-exit signal from the check.","C":"A ParallelAgent that runs five draft and critique pairs concurrently and returns the first response.","D":"A LoopAgent with no limit, relying solely on prose that asks the writer to stop when satisfied."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"LoopAgent runs its sub-agents in order across iterations. A configured limit bounds execution, and implemented termination can stop it earlier. Sequential and parallel designs do not express this feedback loop; a prompt alone does not enforce a bound. Current ADK graph workflows can also express this pattern. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/workflow-agents/loop-agents/"},{"id":"question-347","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An intake workflow routes to one of nine specialists. A specialist may return a request for reclassification, while repeated reclassification must eventually escalate. Which orchestration structure most directly represents these requirements?","answers":{"A":"A graph with conditional routes, explicit reclassification edges, and a bounded escalation path.","B":"A fixed nine-stage sequence in which every specialist receives every request.","C":"A parallel fan-out to all nine specialists followed by concatenation of their outputs.","D":"Direct A2A messaging among specialists, with termination left to whichever agent responds last."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Graph edges express conditional routing and return paths; a bounded escalation path prevents indefinite cycling. A fixed sequence or unconditional fan-out executes unnecessary specialists. A2A standardizes communication but does not define this application's routing and termination policy. Reference: https://adk.dev/graphs/"},{"id":"question-348","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Choose two. An organization is connecting an ADK agent to a supported Google Cloud remote MCP server. Which two setup decisions are required for authorized use?","answers":{"A":"Enable the underlying product API in the project, following that server's setup documentation.","B":"Authenticate the caller and grant required MCP-use and underlying resource permissions with least privilege.","C":"Register the endpoint in Agent Registry instead of granting permissions to the underlying data.","D":"Grant Service Usage Admin to the runtime agent because enabling an API confers access to all of its data."},"correct_answer":["A","B"],"explanation":"Product API enablement and runtime authorization solve different requirements. MCP access does not replace permissions on the resources a tool accesses. Registration does not grant those permissions, and an API-enablement administrator role is not a substitute for narrowly scoped runtime data access. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/enable-disable-mcp-servers ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/control-mcp-use-iam"},{"id":"question-349","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A deployed finance agent must keep using the exact reviewed contents of a reconciliation skill while its owners publish a new default version. The team uses Skill Registry. Which reference should deployment configuration retain?","answers":{"A":"The mutable skill resource, always resolving its default revision at runtime.","B":"The approved immutable Skill revision.","C":"A search query for the most relevant skill by display name and description.","D":"The publisher's name, allowing any skill revision from that publisher to replace the current one."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Skill Registry (Preview) separates a mutable skill and default revision from immutable skill revisions. Pinning the reviewed revision preserves the intended contents while defaults change. Name-based search and publisher trust do not identify an exact reviewed payload; version pinning also does not make execution inherently safe. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/skill-registry"},{"id":"question-350","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A Python ADK application uses a persistent session service. It needs a language preference shared across one user's sessions and scratch data that lasts only for the current invocation. Which state-key scopes fit?","answers":{"A":"Unprefixed language preference and app:-prefixed scratch data.","B":"user:-prefixed language preference and temp:-prefixed scratch data.","C":"app:-prefixed language preference and unprefixed scratch data.","D":"temp:-prefixed language preference and user:-prefixed scratch data."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The user: scope spans a user's sessions within the same application; temp: lasts only for an invocation. Unprefixed keys are session-scoped, while app: is shared across users. Persistence depends on the session service; in-memory state does not survive process restart. Reference: https://adk.dev/sessions/state/"},{"id":"question-351","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team extends Agents CLI with an internal deployment command and supplemental skills. Fresh checkouts and CI must execute the exact reviewed extension code; releases must not advance silently. Which project configuration best fits the documented extension lifecycle?","answers":{"A":"Install a global extension tracking the main branch and omit it from project source control.","B":"Paste the extension's instructions into each developer's system prompt and resolve its code at execution time.","C":"Commit the project-scoped extension pin and vendored contents; review explicit updates to its pin.","D":"Publish only an Agent Registry display name and use it as the command implementation in CI."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Agents CLI extensions use commit-pinned, lockfile-style project configuration with vendored contents. Controlled updates preserve reproducibility. Global configuration is machine-scoped; prompts do not pin executable dependencies; a registry name alone is not an installed CLI extension. Reference: https://google.github.io/agents-cli/guide/extensions/using/"},{"id":"question-352","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Dense retrieval works for conceptual support questions but misses exact identifiers such as VX-4471-B. The corpus also contains paraphrases without those identifiers. Which retrieval design best addresses both query types?","answers":{"A":"Combine dense and sparse/keyword retrieval with evaluated result fusion.","B":"Use only exact keyword matching and discard semantically similar passages without the identifier.","C":"Increase embedding dimensions while preserving the current retrieval method and query handling.","D":"Fine-tune the answer generator on identifiers while retaining the failing candidate retriever."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Hybrid retrieval combines semantic matches with lexical matches for identifiers. Fusion must be evaluated for the workload; it is not an automatic quality guarantee. Keyword-only search sacrifices paraphrase coverage, while larger embeddings or generator tuning do not directly add the missing lexical retrieval path. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/about-hybrid-search"},{"id":"question-353","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An ADK eval case requires check_inventory, check_fraud, and get_shipping_rates with expected arguments. Their order does not matter. Extra calls to an unrelated write tool must still fail evaluation. What should the team configure?","answers":{"A":"Use EXACT matching against one ordering and lower the threshold until all valid permutations pass.","B":"Use ANY_ORDER for the required calls and add a separate check that rejects prohibited extra tool calls.","C":"Use ANY_ORDER alone, since it rejects all tool calls that are absent from the reference trajectory.","D":"Use IN_ORDER so permutations and extra calls are accepted whenever the required tool names eventually appear."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ADK ANY_ORDER requires the expected calls but permits extras. A separate prohibition check is needed for the write tool. EXACT rejects valid permutations, while IN_ORDER preserves reference ordering. Lowering a threshold does not express these semantic requirements. Reference: https://adk.dev/evaluate/criteria/"},{"id":"question-354","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A custom LLM autorater will gate releases for policy-sensitive answers. Its scores appear stable across repeated runs. What additional evidence is most important before trusting it for this use case?","answers":{"A":"A larger judge context window than the candidate model and a low judge temperature on every request.","B":"Agreement with independent expert labels, including difficult cases and systematic disagreements.","C":"Agreement between the candidate self-rating and the autorater on answers generated from the same prompt template.","D":"A high average score for the candidate on synthetic examples written and labeled by that same candidate."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Repeatability measures consistency, not correctness. Human-labeled examples let the team evaluate judge alignment and investigate systematic mistakes. Judge size and temperature do not establish domain validity; self-labeling and shared model biases can make multiple incorrect ratings agree. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluate-judge-model"},{"id":"question-355","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An asynchronous ADK agent on Agent Runtime has low CPU use but long queues during bursts. Traces show most execution time waits for model and tool I/O. Memory and downstream quotas have headroom. Which tuning experiment best targets underused serving capacity?","answers":{"A":"Increase CPU allocation first while preserving the current low per-container request concurrency.","B":"Load-test higher container_concurrency while watching memory, latency, and downstream saturation.","C":"Lower container_concurrency so every I/O wait is isolated in its own serving instance.","D":"Set container_concurrency to the highest accepted value and promote if average CPU remains low."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Asynchronous I/O can allow an instance to serve more concurrent requests. Increasing concurrency may reduce queues, but must be measured because excessive concurrency can exhaust memory or dependencies. CPU scaling and further serialization do not directly address the observed unused I/O capacity. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/optimize-and-scale"},{"id":"question-356","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Offline evaluations pass for a revised agent prompt. The team wants production evidence while limiting exposure to regressions. Which rollout policy is strongest?","answers":{"A":"Switch all requests during a quiet period, and roll back only if the service returns more HTTP errors.","B":"Start with a small representative canary, compare quality and operational metrics to the baseline, and expand only after defined gates pass.","C":"Keep the candidate on a small share indefinitely and expand automatically after a fixed date regardless of observed quality.","D":"Send half the traffic to each version and select the candidate whenever its average response time is lower."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Canarying tests changes under real traffic with limited initial exposure. Predefined quality, latency, reliability, and cost gates determine expansion or rollback. Quiet-hour cutovers expose all active users; fixed dates and a single latency metric ignore the relevant failure criteria. Reference: https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"},{"id":"question-357","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Three agent incidents show these patterns: (1) one tool span dominates latency while turn count is unchanged; (2) repeated successful calls with nearly identical arguments drive up model turns; (3) instruction adherence worsens with long history and recovers when irrelevant history is removed. Which diagnosis best matches the evidence?","answers":{"A":"(1) model drift; (2) tool authentication failure; (3) insufficient runtime replicas.","B":"(1) tool invocation latency; (2) orchestration loop; (3) context-management degradation.","C":"(1) model inference latency; (2) missing retrieval context; (3) downstream API throttling.","D":"(1) runtime cold start; (2) database contention; (3) a model version change."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The dominant span locates the delay; repeated successful actions suggest a loop. Recovery after history reduction supports a context-management cause. These are evidence-based working diagnoses, not access to hidden model reasoning. The other mappings conflict with the observed spans or controlled replay. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/tracing; https://adk.dev/context/compaction/"},{"id":"question-358","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A frozen golden set still passes after a catalog restructure, while sampled production questions about new products fail. The model, code, and prompt are unchanged. How should the evaluation program respond?","answers":{"A":"Replace the historical golden set with only the new failing cases so its score better reflects recent complaints.","B":"Retain the historical regression suite and add reviewed cases representing the new catalog and current query distribution.","C":"Lower the production alert threshold until the unchanged golden-set score and live quality score agree.","D":"Keep the dataset fixed permanently; modifying any benchmark makes meaningful evaluation impossible."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The old suite remains useful for historical regressions but does not cover newly observed traffic. Versioned additions and separate current-traffic slices preserve comparability while improving coverage. Discarding history loses protections, and threshold changes do not repair an unrepresentative dataset. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluation-dataset; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/evaluate-online"},{"id":"question-359","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Choose two. Cost analysis finds a large static preamble repeated across many requests and a high share of simple FAQs sent to an expensive model. Caching is supported for the selected model, and FAQ quality can be evaluated separately. Which two experiments most directly target these cost drivers?","answers":{"A":"Cache reusable context and measure net savings using cache hits, reuse frequency, and any storage charges.","B":"Route simple FAQs to a cheaper model after validating the router and the smaller model on representative cases.","C":"Shorten the output allowance for every task, including cases that need long structured responses, without re-evaluation.","D":"Increase serving replicas so each model invocation has more CPU capacity available for prompt processing."},"correct_answer":["A","B"],"explanation":"Caching can discount reused input but does not make processing or storage universally free. Validated routing can avoid unnecessary model expense. Blanket output truncation risks incomplete results, and application replicas do not change the managed model price per token. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/context-cache/context-cache-overview; https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/cost-optimization"},{"id":"question-360","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent incorrectly issues refunds when users ask hypothetical questions. The team fixes the behavior and adds tool-side controls. Which update to evaluation assets best helps prevent recurrence?","answers":{"A":"Keep only the exact production transcript as a manually executed test before major releases.","B":"Add paired hypothetical/explicit-refund cases, including assertions that no refund tool is called.","C":"Add the new rule to the judging prompt and remove existing refund examples that now fail under the changed rubric.","D":"Score whether the agent apologizes after an incorrect refund, since that is the final user-visible outcome."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Regression cases should verify both restraint and valid execution. Contrasting hypothetical requests with explicit actions tests the decision boundary and catches overcorrection. Manual-only testing is inconsistent; changing the judge or evaluating apologies does not prove that prohibited actions stopped. Reference: https://codelabs.developers.google.com/adk-eval/instructions; https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-361","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A team wants ongoing evidence that production RAG responses are supported by the information available to the agent. What design best supports that objective?","answers":{"A":"Re-run a generic model-provider benchmark quarterly and apply its score to every deployed RAG configuration.","B":"Sample production traces with responses and relevant context, run groundedness evaluation, and review trends and expert-calibrated alerts.","C":"Count responses containing citations as grounded, even when the cited passages were not captured for evaluation.","D":"Use successful HTTP status codes and low latency as proxies for supported answers between annual audits."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Online evaluation can asynchronously score live traces, provided the needed telemetry is exported. Assessing support requires the relevant context and response. Citations alone can be incorrect, infrastructure metrics do not measure groundedness, and external benchmarks do not represent this agent's traffic. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/evaluate-online"},{"id":"question-362","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Choose two. A team is considering a third-party MCP server for its coding agents. The server will execute locally and can access an external service. Which two review decisions reduce distinct risks?","answers":{"A":"Review its tool descriptions and returned content as possible sources of instructions that could redirect the agent away from the authorized task.","B":"Approve all tools once the server's transport uses TLS, because transport encryption establishes that tool actions are appropriate.","C":"Run the server with only required credentials, filesystem access, and network destinations, after reviewing its implementation and dependencies.","D":"Give the server the developer's normal credentials and rely on the coding agent's read-only task description to constrain remote changes."},"correct_answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Untrusted integration content can steer an agent, while executable server code has its own permissions and dependencies. Vetting and restricted execution address both surfaces. TLS protects transport, and a read-only prompt does not make broad credentials read-only. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"},{"id":"question-363","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Antigravity tasks run for several hours while reviewers work elsewhere. Reviewers need to understand the proposed approach, inspect actual changes, and see verification evidence at milestones. What should the workflow emphasize?","answers":{"A":"Store complete terminal logs and require reviewers to reconstruct the proposed design from the order of commands.","B":"Use implementation plans, diffs, and walkthrough artifacts with review checkpoints and recorded verification results.","C":"Record only the final natural-language response because intermediate plans can change during implementation.","D":"Publish the agent's token usage and elapsed time for each task, with links to the branch containing its final files."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Artifacts provide structured deliverables for asynchronous review. Raw logs lack a concise design and evidence trail, final prose alone omits inspectable changes, and usage metrics do not establish implementation correctness. Review pauses depend on the configured workflow. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/artifacts/"},{"id":"question-364","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Platform engineering needs (P) managed developer workspaces with retained working files and centrally configured tools, and (Q) a nightly fleet of short-lived coding-agent tasks on an existing GKE cluster. Which mapping minimizes custom environment management?","answers":{"A":"P: Cloud Workstations. Q: Kubernetes Jobs using appropriately isolated GKE Sandbox Pods, with per-task access and cleanup.","B":"P: Long-running GKE Jobs with hand-built IDE access. Q: One permanently assigned Cloud Workstation per nightly task.","C":"P: A shared Compute Engine desktop for all developers. Q: Containers sharing that desktop's home directory and credentials.","D":"P: Cloud Workstations. Q: One shared unrestricted GKE Pod where every agent edits the same checkout in parallel."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Cloud Workstations provides centrally managed development environments; Jobs fit finite batch tasks on an existing cluster. Shared desktops and checkouts weaken separation, and permanently assigned workspaces add unnecessary batch-management overhead. GKE security controls must still be configured. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/overview"},{"id":"question-365","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity team needs three customizations: project-wide coding guidance, an on-demand migration procedure with helper scripts, and a linter invoked after designated editing tools finish. Which mapping is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Coding guidance: skill; migration procedure: Always On rule; post-edit linter: research subagent.","B":"Coding guidance: Always On rule; migration procedure: skill; post-edit linter: matching PostToolUse hook.","C":"Coding guidance: PreToolUse hook; migration procedure: MCP server description; post-edit linter: manual rule.","D":"Coding guidance: plugin manifest; migration procedure: PostInvocation hook; post-edit linter: skill description."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Rules supply guidance, skills package procedures, and hooks execute handlers at configured events. A post-tool hook must match the relevant editing tools; it does not automatically cover every filesystem mutation. Rules and skills are not hard access-control boundaries. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/hooks/"},{"id":"question-366","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team will run Antigravity CLI in CI to review pull requests, consult an approved standards MCP server, and save review results. No person is available to answer interactive tool prompts. Which setup best meets the requirement?","answers":{"A":"Use headless mode with pre-established authentication, narrowly allowed tools, sandbox restrictions, a timeout, and captured structured results checked by CI.","B":"Use headless mode with default Ask permissions and treat a zero process exit code as proof that every requested review tool executed.","C":"Use an interactive terminal session inside the runner and wait for repository maintainers to answer permission prompts when they appear.","D":"Use headless mode with every permission bypassed and a developer's full credentials so the review cannot be delayed by authorization failures."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Headless automation needs authentication and allowed actions established beforehand. Approval-dependent tools can be soft-denied, so CI must validate required work. Interactive sessions defeat unattended operation; blanket permission bypass and broad credentials are unnecessary. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/headless/"},{"id":"question-367","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team has a working agent prototype and wants Agents CLI to add deployment configuration and CI/CD while preserving existing application logic. Which operation is the best starting point?","answers":{"A":"Create a new empty agent project with agents-cli scaffold create and replace the current repository with the generated files.","B":"Use agents-cli scaffold enhance on the existing project and review the proposed infrastructure changes.","C":"Run agents-cli update to refresh installed coding-agent skills and assume the application repository is productionized.","D":"Publish the current prototype to Agent Registry and use the registry entry as the generated deployment pipeline."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Scaffold enhance adds production infrastructure to an existing project. Initializing a new project targets a different starting point, updating refreshes installed skills, and publishing catalogs an agent rather than supplying its missing CI/CD configuration. Reference: https://google.github.io/agents-cli/cli/"},{"id":"question-368","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A checkout service's CPU usage has risen, and an agent is asked to optimize its runtime without changing behavior. Read-only production profiles are available; code execution occurs in a test sandbox. Which workflow provides the strongest evidence?","answers":{"A":"Have the agent optimize functions with the most source lines, then compare the new source size with the original implementation.","B":"Inspect profiles, establish a representative benchmark, make a targeted change, then compare performance and regression results before review.","C":"Benchmark only a small function selected by the model and ship once its isolated runtime improves, regardless of request-level behavior.","D":"Add caches to repeated calls and evaluate the change by reduced database traffic, deferring correctness and memory checks to production."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Profiles identify actual resource consumers; representative before-and-after measurements and tests assess benefit and preserved behavior. Code size is not a bottleneck measure, isolated microbenchmarks may mislead, and caching can introduce correctness or memory problems. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler"},{"id":"question-369","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A scheduled agent performs application-owned maintenance against an external API that supports the OAuth client-credentials grant. It also reads Google Cloud resources on its own authority. No end user is involved. Which credential design is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Use a platform administrator's three-legged OAuth refresh token for both destinations.","B":"Use external client credentials and the agent's own identity with least-privilege Google Cloud IAM.","C":"Use a client-credentials token from the external API as an interchangeable credential for Google Cloud APIs.","D":"Use user-delegated OAuth for the external API and allow unauthenticated Google Cloud access from the runtime network."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The external service's client-credentials grant represents the application, while Google Cloud access uses the agent identity and IAM. An administrator's user token creates unnecessary delegation. Tokens issued for one authorization domain are not interchangeable, and network location does not remove authentication requirements. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/agent-identity-overview"},{"id":"question-370","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent's PAB makes it eligible to access a Cloud Storage bucket. Policy inspection confirms that no deny policy applies, but no IAM allow binding grants the agent permission to read the objects. The read fails. What should you do?","answers":{"A":"Remove the PAB because eligible resources are inaccessible until the boundary is deleted.","B":"Grant a scoped IAM role containing the required read permission.","C":"Add an identical PAB rule to turn resource eligibility into an object-read grant.","D":"Enable Cloud Storage audit logging, which authorizes otherwise eligible reads."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A PAB limits eligibility; an IAM allow policy supplies permissions. An applicable deny would still take precedence if present. Duplicating the boundary or enabling logging cannot grant access, and deleting the boundary unnecessarily weakens the restriction. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/policy-types"},{"id":"question-371","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A platform must enforce Model Armor screening on supported model and MCP traffic from Agent Runtime. Several teams omit application-level screening calls. Which architecture makes the screening requirement enforceable across those workloads?","answers":{"A":"Publish a shared screening package and measure how many repositories declare it as a dependency.","B":"Configure Agent Gateway content policies for the supported traffic paths, require those paths, and prevent alternative routes that bypass the controls.","C":"Create Model Armor templates and rely on their existence to intercept every outbound connection automatically.","D":"Register the models and tools, then rely only on registry discovery permissions to inspect their request bodies."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Screening must be configured on the actual traffic path and bypasses must be controlled. Gateway can delegate content decisions to Model Armor; registration and template creation alone do not inspect traffic. Dependency declarations do not establish that screening runs for every invocation. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/govern/gateways/set-up-agent-gateway"},{"id":"question-372","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent is subject to an organization principal-set PAB allowing resources in projects alpha and beta. You add another applicable PAB allowing only alpha. The agent retains an IAM read grant in beta and still reads a protected beta resource. Assuming the permission is covered by PAB enforcement, why?","answers":{"A":"The new policy has lower priority because organization PAB policies override project PAB policies.","B":"PAB resource eligibility is a union; remove this agent's eligibility under the broader applicable policy.","C":"PAB policies combine by intersection, but the service gives an existing IAM allow binding precedence over the newer boundary.","D":"Adding a second PAB disables all boundary evaluation until the agent is redeployed."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A resource remains eligible when any applicable PAB includes it. Adding a narrower policy does not subtract eligibility from a broader policy. Reducing eligibility requires safely changing the relevant policies or bindings while keeping a boundary in effect; precedence and redeployment do not explain this result. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/principal-access-boundary-policies-remove"},{"id":"question-373","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent legitimately needs a sensitive-data lookup tool and an external-message tool, but policy forbids their use in a risky combination within one execution. Static IAM permissions allow both tools individually. Which additional control is most appropriate?","answers":{"A":"A larger model context window so the agent is more likely to remember the policy.","B":"Enforce context-aware execution policies, using Gateway delegation to Semantic Governance policies.","C":"A principal access boundary listing the agent's project, which evaluates the semantic relationship between tool calls.","D":"An Agent Registry description warning that these two tools should not be combined."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Context-aware governance addresses combinations that individually valid tool permissions cannot express. Gateway supports delegation to Semantic Governance policies for this purpose. PAB controls resource eligibility, while context size and registry descriptions do not enforce execution policy. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/govern/gateways/agent-gateway-overview"},{"id":"question-374","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A company is adopting Agent Registry to govern a growing fleet. A team claims that registering an MCP server automatically authorizes every agent to invoke it and guarantees that its code has passed a security review. Which assessment is correct?","answers":{"A":"Registration grants invocation rights, but administrators must separately approve the server's model choice.","B":"Registration provides discoverable metadata for governance; invocation permissions and the organization's trust-review process must still be configured separately.","C":"Registration performs source-code certification, but the server must be registered again for each user session.","D":"Agent Registry is only a billing inventory and cannot support capability discovery or gateway policy scope."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Registry centralizes agent and tool metadata and supports discovery and governance. Being listed is not a blanket invocation grant or a certification of server code. Runtime authorization and supplier review remain separate responsibilities; the registry also provides more than billing inventory. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/agent-registry/overview"},{"id":"question-375","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"Two teams want low-code implementations. M has a tested Dialogflow CX authentication flow with ordered data collection and explicit error routes, which it wants to reuse in a new customer-service application. N needs an employee knowledge assistant over connected enterprise documents. Which pairing best fits?","answers":{"A":"M: Rebuild authentication as Agent Designer prompt instructions. N: Use Agent Designer with connected enterprise documents.","B":"M: Invoke the existing flow through a CX Agent Studio flow-based agent. N: Use Gemini Enterprise Agent Designer with the required connected data sources.","C":"M: Invoke the existing flow through a flow-based agent. N: Put document titles in Agent Designer instructions without connecting their contents.","D":"M: Import the Dialogflow CX flow directly as an executable Agent Designer workflow. N: Use Agent Designer with connected enterprise documents."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CX Agent Studio can reuse an existing Dialogflow CX flow, and Agent Designer supports connected enterprise knowledge. Prompt instructions do not preserve the existing executable flow, nor is direct Dialogflow flow execution an Agent Designer import feature. Document titles alone do not supply the knowledge corpus. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/flow; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/workflow-builder/create-chat-agent"},{"id":"question-376","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A third-party connector has indexed documents and their group ACLs successfully. An employee was removed from a source-system group but can still retrieve that group's documents in Gemini Enterprise. Entity incremental syncs are current; the last identity sync predates the removal. What should you check first?","answers":{"A":"Whether the document chunk size is retaining excerpts from neighboring files with different ACLs.","B":"Whether the identity synchronization has refreshed the employee's group membership.","C":"Whether the answer-generation temperature is allowing the model to ignore the employee's access restrictions.","D":"Whether the connector's content synchronization frequency is high enough to re-embed unchanged documents."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Identity synchronization updates membership used with group ACLs. A current entity incremental sync does not establish that identity data is current. Chunking, re-embedding unchanged content, and generation settings do not refresh group membership. Missing identity mappings should not be described as automatically granting everyone access. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/introduction-to-connectors-and-data-stores"},{"id":"question-377","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Gemini Enterprise assistant's product manuals are imported from Cloud Storage once. New and revised manuals are added daily, but users only find the original versions. A daily refresh meets the business requirement. Which setup removes the need to recreate the data store manually?","answers":{"A":"Schedule a daily prompt update that lists the new file names without changing the ingestion configuration.","B":"Configure the Cloud Storage connector for periodic daily synchronization.","C":"Increase the maximum answer context so the assistant can locate files that are outside the existing index.","D":"Schedule a daily model refresh and preserve the one-time document import to avoid changing the search schema."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Periodic Cloud Storage synchronization refreshes the data store from its source. Prompt changes, larger context, and model refreshes do not import newly published files. Refresh configuration should be selected for the source; incremental import semantics should not be assumed to remove deleted records. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/connect-cloud-storage"},{"id":"question-378","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An Agent Search data store will support answers over PDF manuals whose key information is in annotated diagrams and tables. Digital text extraction omitted these elements in a pilot. Which document-processing configuration should the architect evaluate for the new data store?","answers":{"A":"Enable document chunking and layout parsing with image and table annotation.","B":"Keep digital parsing and increase the number of retrieved chunks until diagrams appear in the answer.","C":"Use only OCR, disable chunking, and treat the recognized characters as a complete representation of each diagram.","D":"Generate embeddings from the PDF filenames and use table captions as metadata filters during retrieval."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Layout parsing preserves document structure; image and table annotations provide descriptive content associated with chunks. Higher retrieval counts cannot recover information absent from the index. OCR alone can miss visual relationships, and filenames do not represent the manuals' substantive content. Validate extraction quality on representative diagrams. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/parse-chunk-documents"},{"id":"question-379","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An Agent Designer refund assistant has access to both physical-product and digital-product policies. Its five few-shot examples all concern physical products, and it now applies that policy to digital purchases. Which prompt revision best addresses this unintended generalization?","answers":{"A":"Add more examples of physical refunds with different answer lengths so the model sees greater stylistic variation.","B":"Use examples covering both product types and missing-type cases, with instructions to identify the type before selecting the applicable policy.","C":"Remove product-type labels from the examples so their answer format appears independent of the product being discussed.","D":"Keep the examples and tell the model that the latest user message always overrides the refund policies supplied by retrieval."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Varied examples and explicit selection criteria address the misleading pattern. A missing-type example can demonstrate asking for clarification. More stylistic variation does not fix category coverage; removing the distinction hides the decision boundary. User requests cannot replace the authoritative refund policy. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/prompts/few-shot-examples"},{"id":"question-380","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK agent confuses three tools that read customer details, update customer details, and close accounts. Traces show incorrect tool selection before any argument validation runs. Their descriptions all say 'customer operation.' What should the team change first?","answers":{"A":"Increase sampling temperature to encourage the agent to explore alternative tool choices.","B":"Give the tools distinct names and descriptions covering purpose, required inputs, and side effects.","C":"Retain the descriptions but retry a different tool whenever the first operation completes successfully.","D":"Replace the tools with an unrestricted customer-operation string and let the backend infer the action."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Tool metadata helps the model distinguish available actions. Precise descriptions address the observed selection problem; execution authorization remains necessary. Higher temperature or blind retries do not establish correct intent, while a freeform operation interface loses useful structure. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-381","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A reporting tool returns up to 200,000 database rows, although the agent normally needs a regional total and a few exceptions. Tool responses consume the model's context budget. Which interface redesign best supports both summaries and occasional drill-down?","answers":{"A":"Return every row as compressed base64 and require the model to decompress it in context.","B":"Expose bounded aggregation and filtered pagination, with references for retrieving further detail.","C":"Raise the model's output-token limit to make additional room for tool-result input.","D":"Return a fixed sample of the first ten rows as if it represented the complete report."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Bounded, task-specific results reduce context use while preserving a path to detail. Encoded bulk data still needs processing; output limits do not increase input capacity. An unlabeled fixed sample can misrepresent totals and omit exceptions rather than compute the requested result. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-382","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A reporting agent should have no database write capability. Its SQL tool currently connects as a database owner and rejects requests containing DELETE or DROP with a string check. Which design provides the strongest enforcement?","answers":{"A":"Keep the owner credential and add examples of disallowed SQL to the system instruction.","B":"Enforce database read-only privileges and expose validated, parameterized read tools.","C":"Allow the owner credential only when the model reports high confidence that the SQL is read-only.","D":"Log attempted changes and run a compensating write if an auditor later flags a violation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Enforce the capability boundary at the database and constrain the tool interface. Keyword checks can miss other writes or indirect execution paths. Prompts and self-reported confidence are not authorization controls; retrospective compensation does not prevent unauthorized modification. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"},{"id":"question-383","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK agent produces billing instructions for a JSON-consuming service. Prose sometimes surrounds the JSON, and occasionally a syntactically valid amount violates a business rule. Which design handles both failures?","answers":{"A":"Require 'JSON only' in the prompt and trust any response that contains opening and closing braces.","B":"Constrain JSON with a supported schema, then validate billing rules before execution.","C":"Strip prose with a regular expression and submit all parseable values to the billing service.","D":"Set temperature to zero and treat repeatable output as sufficient evidence of valid billing instructions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Structured output constrains supported syntax and schema fields, but does not establish factual or business correctness. Validate amounts and authorization before execution, and handle incomplete or failed responses. Regex extraction, deterministic sampling, and prompt wording do not replace these checks. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/capabilities/control-generated-output"},{"id":"question-384","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A public-news agent must use recent web information. A separate internal-engineering agent must answer from approved private documents accessible to the requesting employee. Which grounding strategy best respects these knowledge boundaries?","answers":{"A":"Google Search grounding for news; authorized retrieval over the internal corpus for engineering.","B":"Google Search grounding for both, with private document names included in the engineering query.","C":"A monthly fine-tune for news; unrestricted organization-wide document retrieval for engineering.","D":"One shared retrieval pool combining public pages and private documents, with source selection left entirely to the generator."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Public Search grounding supplies web evidence; private-document answers need a retrieval path with the proper access controls. Search cannot gain private access from document names. Fine-tuning is not a current-news feed, and unrestricted or mixed retrieval can violate the internal agent's source boundary. Reference: https://adk.dev/grounding/google_search_grounding/ ; https://adk.dev/grounding/grounding_with_search/"},{"id":"question-385","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Product prices and stock change throughout the day. The agent must verify both before placing an order. The team proposes monthly catalog fine-tuning and a nightly search-index refresh. Which architecture best meets the freshness requirement?","answers":{"A":"Retain monthly tuning and use lower temperature to stabilize the model's recalled catalog facts.","B":"Retrieve descriptions; verify price and inventory through authoritative APIs before checkout.","C":"Keep the nightly index as the sole source because retrieval inherently guarantees current data.","D":"Store each user's last observed prices in Memory Bank and treat them as current at checkout."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Retrieval freshness depends on ingestion and caching; it is not automatically real time. An authoritative read at the decision point addresses volatile price and stock. Weight updates and remembered observations age between changes; lower temperature does not refresh them. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/rag-overview ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/overview"},{"id":"question-386","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team changes query embeddings from model X to unrelated model Y but keeps document vectors from X. Both models produce vectors of equal length. Quality collapses. What migration design addresses the incompatibility?","answers":{"A":"Re-embed documents with Y and switch matching query and index versions together after validation.","B":"Keep the corpus vectors and raise top-k until the ranking becomes stable across repeated calls.","C":"Pad query vectors with zeros and change the index's shard count without re-embedding documents.","D":"Retain both models in one unversioned index and normalize every vector to unit length."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Matching dimension counts do not imply matching embedding spaces. Unless compatibility is explicitly documented, queries and documents must use the same compatible embedding setup. Increasing candidate count, padding, or normalization cannot align unrelated learned coordinate systems; versioned migration avoids mixed-space serving. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/embeddings/get-text-embeddings"},{"id":"question-387","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Answers often omit a policy exception because its paragraph falls immediately after the rule in a different fixed-size chunk. The source document contains useful section headings. Which ingestion experiment most directly addresses this failure while retaining passage-level retrieval?","answers":{"A":"Index every entire policy manual as one vector, regardless of its length or topic diversity.","B":"Chunk by document structure and evaluate modest overlap around rule-and-exception boundaries.","C":"Reduce all chunks to 30 tokens so each retrieved unit is as specific as possible.","D":"Duplicate each existing chunk under a new ID to increase its chance of appearing in results."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Structure-aware chunks and measured overlap can retain rule-and-exception context. Whole manuals reduce passage specificity and may exceed embedding limits; tiny chunks increase fragmentation. Duplicating unchanged chunks adds redundancy without restoring the missing relationship. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/layout-parser-integration"},{"id":"question-388","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A custom Vector Search retriever must restrict results to the authenticated tenant's product edition and version before semantic ranking. These attributes are stored with each vector. Which implementation fits?","answers":{"A":"Encode edition and version only in embedding text and rely on semantic proximity to enforce eligibility.","B":"Apply server-derived metadata restrictions to the vector query, then rank within the eligible subset.","C":"Retrieve globally and let the generator hide ineligible text after reading all returned chunks.","D":"Let the model supply a tenant identifier and trust it because the request came through the agent."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Metadata restrictions express structured eligibility conditions that vector similarity cannot enforce. Derive tenant scope from trusted authentication and enforce authorization before content reaches the model. Global retrieval followed by prompt filtering exposes ineligible data; model-provided identifiers are not proof of tenancy. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/filtering"},{"id":"question-389","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A user asks about audit-log retention and then asks, 'And on the Pro plan?' Searching only the follow-up text yields unrelated results. The preceding turn unambiguously identifies the topic. Which pre-retrieval step is most appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Rewrite the follow-up into a standalone query that preserves the audit-log topic and Pro-plan constraint.","B":"Concatenate every previous conversation with the user into one embedding input for maximum context.","C":"Search only for 'Pro' and ask the generator to infer the relevant product feature from returned passages.","D":"Skip retrieval for the follow-up and answer from the model's general understanding of subscription plans."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Context-aware query rewriting resolves the omitted topic before retrieval while preserving the new constraint. An entire history can dilute the query or exceed limits; searching a plan name is underspecified. Skipping retrieval abandons the evidence requirement. If context were ambiguous, clarification would be appropriate. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/answer"},{"id":"question-390","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An A2A partner accepts a report task that may run for 20 minutes. Your request path times out after 60 seconds, and you cannot assume push notifications are supported. How should the client track completion without creating duplicate work?","answers":{"A":"Resubmit the report request with a new message ID every minute until a report arrives.","B":"Track the task ID and poll status; optionally use supported streaming or push notifications.","C":"Treat the initial accepted task as completed and generate the missing report locally.","D":"Require a permanently open synchronous connection and discard task identifiers when it disconnects."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A2A provides task tracking independently of the initial request. Polling is usable without push capability; streaming and push depend on support and configuration. Resubmitting new work risks duplication, acceptance is not completion, and connection lifetime should not be the sole record of task progress. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/streaming-and-async/ ; https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/#313-get-task"},{"id":"question-391","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A vendor offers a remote capability that plans its own investigation, may ask follow-up questions, and returns artifacts after long-running work. Clients use different agent frameworks. Why is an A2A interface a good fit?","answers":{"A":"It makes the vendor's internal prompts, tools, and model state available for clients to modify.","B":"It standardizes agent capabilities, task progress, messages, and artifacts across implementations.","C":"It eliminates the need for authentication because clients discover an Agent Card before connecting.","D":"It guarantees lower latency than a purpose-built REST endpoint for every invocation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A2A models interactions with independently implemented agents, including stateful work and outputs. It does not require exposing implementation internals, grant unauthenticated access, or promise a transport-latency advantage. A simple API may be sufficient for a fixed operation without those interaction needs. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/key-concepts/"},{"id":"question-392","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An MCP server must expose ticket creation as an invocable operation. It must also expose an on-call schedule as a URI-addressed document the host application can list and read for context. Which capability mapping matches these explicit interface requirements?","answers":{"A":"Two tools, with both ticket creation and document listing available only through tools/call.","B":"A ticket-creation tool and an on-call schedule resource.","C":"A ticket-creation resource and an on-call prompt that performs the schedule read as a side effect.","D":"Two prompts, using prompt expansion to commit tickets and retrieve the schedule document."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Tools expose callable operations; resources expose URI-addressed contextual content through resource interfaces. A read-only operation can also be modeled as a tool, but that would not meet this question's explicit resource listing and reading requirement. Prompts are not an execution mechanism for committing tickets. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/resources ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/overview"},{"id":"question-393","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A local stdio MCP prototype must become a shared service used by many agent hosts. The team needs authenticated remote access and centralized operation. Which transport architecture is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Expose raw stdin and stdout over a public TCP port and retain local-process trust assumptions.","B":"Host an MCP server using Streamable HTTP with appropriate authentication and authorization.","C":"Share one long-lived local process by copying its environment variables into every agent host.","D":"Replace MCP with an unauthenticated REST endpoint because MCP is limited to same-machine processes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"MCP supports stdio for local subprocess communication and Streamable HTTP for remote servers. Remote use needs explicit security and lifecycle management. Raw stdio over TCP and copied credentials are not that architecture; replacing the protocol or dropping authentication is unnecessary. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports"},{"id":"question-394","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK coordinator should consult a specialist, receive its result, and then continue composing the final answer itself. The specialist should behave like a callable capability rather than take ownership of the conversation. Which integration best expresses that control flow?","answers":{"A":"Transfer conversation control to the specialist and require it to produce the final user response.","B":"Wrap the specialist in AgentTool and register it as a coordinator tool.","C":"Put coordinator and specialist in a ParallelAgent that returns whichever response finishes first.","D":"Write the specialist's name into shared state and assume the runner will call it automatically."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"AgentTool lets a parent invoke another agent and consume its result as a tool response. Transferring control has different conversational semantics. Parallel execution does not represent this consult-and-return dependency, and a state value does not itself invoke an agent. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-395","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"In an ADK sequential workflow, an extraction agent produces order details that the next agent needs. The application also requires those details to persist through its session service. Which handoff follows ADK's state mechanism?","answers":{"A":"Mutate a separately fetched Session object's state dictionary without appending any event or tracked state change.","B":"Persist the extraction through output_key or tracked context-state updates, and have the next step read that key.","C":"Place the extracted details in a module-global dictionary indexed only by agent name.","D":"Return the details in an unconsumed debug log and expect the next agent to recover them automatically."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ADK tracks supported state updates through events; output_key stores an agent result for subsequent steps. Arbitrary mutation of a fetched Session can bypass persistence. Globals risk cross-session contamination, and logs are not an automatic state handoff. Reference: https://adk.dev/sessions/state/"},{"id":"question-396","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An FAQ agent using one authorized document corpus meets the team's quality target in testing. A proposed five-agent planner/researcher/writer/critic/publisher design has no demonstrated quality advantage and exceeds the latency budget. What should the architect recommend?","answers":{"A":"Adopt all five agents because additional specialization necessarily improves grounded-answer accuracy.","B":"Keep the validated single-agent retrieval design.","C":"Retain five agents but remove retrieval to recover the added orchestration latency.","D":"Replace the evaluated design with a static keyword lookup before checking whether it meets conversational requirements."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The measured requirements favor the simpler design. Additional agents add coordination and model calls and should have a demonstrated purpose. Removing retrieval compromises evidence, while an unevaluated replacement does not establish that the existing requirements are met. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/routing/"},{"id":"question-397","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A verification workflow requires identity checks, eligibility checks, and final approval in that order. Approval must not run if either check fails. Which orchestration design most reliably represents this requirement?","answers":{"A":"Give all steps to an LLM planner and request that it avoid skipping checks.","B":"Use deterministic ordering with explicit success conditions that block or route failures before approval.","C":"Run every step in parallel and ask the final agent to disregard premature approval results.","D":"Use a sequential list but catch all check failures as empty outputs and continue to approval."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Fixed ordering should be encoded structurally, and failed prerequisites need explicit control-flow handling. A sequential list alone does not make every business check succeed. Model planning, premature parallel approval, and swallowed errors do not enforce the required gate. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/workflow-agents/sequential-agents/ ; https://adk.dev/graphs/"},{"id":"question-398","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A high-volume agent handles routine classification well with a small model, but a measured subset requires a stronger model. A validated router identifies that subset within the latency budget. Which design uses this evidence to lower serving cost?","answers":{"A":"Invoke the stronger model for every request and ask it to keep its internal reasoning brief.","B":"Apply the validated model router and monitor its quality and routing errors.","C":"Select a model randomly for each request so both endpoints receive comparable traffic.","D":"Use the small model for all requests and equate low cost with sufficient quality."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Task-aware routing can allocate expensive capacity to cases that benefit from it. Its benefit depends on router and endpoint quality, so evaluate the combined system. Random routing ignores task requirements, and using either model universally discards the measured cost-quality trade-off. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/models/routing/"},{"id":"question-399","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A self-hosted open model's weight memory exceeds the team's preferred GPU capacity. The application can tolerate a small measured quality loss, and the serving engine supports lower-precision kernels. Which experiment most directly targets weight memory?","answers":{"A":"Evaluate a supported quantized model and measure end-to-end quality, memory, and latency.","B":"Increase the context window and batch size while retaining the existing weight precision.","C":"Lower sampling temperature while retaining the same checkpoint and serving configuration.","D":"Enable a response cache and assume that the model no longer needs its weights loaded for cache misses."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Quantization reduces weight precision and can reduce memory requirements. Quality and speed depend on the model, kernels, hardware, and workload; improvement is not guaranteed. Larger context and batches can increase memory, sampling settings do not compress weights, and cache misses still require inference. Reference: https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/core"},{"id":"question-400","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An order agent must reserve stock and insert an order atomically in an existing PostgreSQL system of record. The company wants managed database operations and must preserve relational constraints. Which integration best fits?","answers":{"A":"A narrow tool calling Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL, with both changes in one database transaction.","B":"Two independent Memory Bank writes, one remembering the reservation and one remembering the order.","C":"A Vector Search update followed by an order-status prompt that reconciles partial failures.","D":"Separate uncoordinated writes to a Redis cache, treating cache acknowledgement as ledger commit."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Cloud SQL preserves the existing relational database model and its transactional operations. Keep the business transaction in the database-backed tool. Extracted memories and vector entries are not relational ledger transactions; independent cache writes do not enforce the stated system-of-record invariant. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/introduction ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/manage-connections"},{"id":"question-401","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A custom agent application stores variable-shape job documents that workers must resume after process restart. Two workers may compete to claim the same job. The team chooses Firestore. How should it implement the claim?","answers":{"A":"Use a Firestore transaction that reads the current claim state and conditionally records the worker's claim.","B":"Keep each claim in the worker's in-memory ADK session and reconcile workers after the job finishes.","C":"Read the document, then blindly overwrite its owner field outside any transaction or precondition.","D":"Store the job as an extracted memory and infer the owner from whichever worker retrieves it first."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Firestore transactions coordinate a read-dependent update and can retry on contention. Durable job state survives a worker restart. In-memory state and blind overwrites cannot enforce a single claim; extracted memory is not a work-claim protocol. Claim expiry and recovery still need application design. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/firestore/native/docs/manage-data/transactions"},{"id":"question-402","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team wants to include its entire multi-gigabyte engineering corpus in every request because its selected model supports a long context window. Most questions require only a few sections. Which architecture should be the starting point?","answers":{"A":"Include the entire corpus and assume the advertised context window removes token, latency, and access-control concerns.","B":"Retrieve authorized, relevant passages within a context budget, using long context when a task benefits from more evidence.","C":"Remove source documents from the workflow and rely entirely on model pretraining to eliminate input cost.","D":"Use one permanent summary of the entire corpus for all future questions, regardless of document changes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Retrieval selects task-relevant evidence from corpora larger than practical request budgets. Long context can complement it but does not remove cost, capacity, freshness, or authorization requirements. Pretraining and a permanent summary can omit or stale the evidence required for a particular question. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/rag-engine/rag-overview"},{"id":"question-403","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK application's prompt builder serializes session state into model input, and traces record that input. A developer places a user's OAuth refresh token in a general state key for tools to reuse. Which redesign protects the credential while preserving delegated access?","answers":{"A":"Keep the token in that state key but instruct the model never to repeat it.","B":"Keep credentials in the managed auth layer and only a nonsecret reference in general state.","C":"Base64-encode the token in state and decode it inside the prompt template before use.","D":"Replace every user's token with one broad shared SaaS administrator credential in the tool code."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"This application's serialization path would expose the token to prompts and traces. An authentication service can manage delegated credentials and provide tools with access when needed. Prompt instructions or encoding do not protect a disclosed secret; an administrator credential changes the authorization model rather than fixing custody. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/auth-with-3lo"},{"id":"question-404","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A multi-tenant application generates Memory Bank memories and accepts a client-supplied user ID for retrieval. An attacker changes that ID to retrieve another user's preferences. Which correction addresses the authorization flaw?","answers":{"A":"Retain the ID parameter but increase the memory relevance threshold before returning results.","B":"Enforce identity-derived memory scope in the backend and applicable IAM Conditions.","C":"Put all memories in an application-wide scope so the client no longer needs to choose a user ID.","D":"Hide user IDs in the UI while leaving the backend retrieval parameter unrestricted."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Memory scope partitions data, but callers must be authorized for the requested scope. Bind scope selection to trusted identity and enforce access in the backend and supported IAM policy. Similarity thresholds and hidden UI fields are not authorization; a shared scope expands exposure. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/memory-bank/iam-conditions"},{"id":"question-405","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An agent repeatedly reads a costly inventory summary. The summary may be at most 30 seconds old for display, but checkout must verify current inventory in Cloud SQL. Which role should Memorystore for Redis play?","answers":{"A":"Replace Cloud SQL as the permanent order ledger and use cached summaries as checkout authority.","B":"Cache scoped summary results with a suitable TTL or invalidation strategy; verify inventory against Cloud SQL at checkout.","C":"Store a single summary indefinitely and use the language model to estimate subsequent inventory changes.","D":"Cache only final natural-language replies without including tenant, query parameters, or data freshness in the cache design."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Memorystore suits frequently reused low-latency data. Cache keys and freshness policy must fit the request and tenant; checkout still uses the authoritative system. An indefinite or improperly scoped cache can return stale or cross-tenant results, and inferred changes are not inventory verification. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/redis/memorystore-for-redis-overview"},{"id":"question-406","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A Python ADK instruction provider receives ReadonlyContext. A developer attempts to write workflow state through it and, after that fails, proposes storing the value in a global variable. Which correction follows the current context API?","answers":{"A":"Keep instruction generation read-only and perform the tracked state update in a supported tool or callback Context.","B":"Cast ReadonlyContext.state to an unrestricted dictionary and assume the session service will persist changes.","C":"Use a process-global variable shared by every user so the instruction provider can mutate it without an error.","D":"Modify the invocation ID and store the desired state value inside that identifier for the next turn."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"ReadonlyContext is intentionally nonmutating. Current Python ADK uses Context for supported callback and tool operations, including tracked state changes. Casting or global variables bypass that mechanism and can introduce cross-session errors; invocation IDs identify runs rather than store application state. Reference: https://adk.dev/context/"},{"id":"question-407","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team wants to reuse an approved contract-summarization agent owned by another team. They need to find its capability description and endpoint before requesting access. Which system should they query first?","answers":{"A":"Agent Registry.","B":"Memory Bank.","C":"Cloud Trace.","D":"Vector Search."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Agent Registry catalogs discoverable agent capabilities and endpoints, including cross-team reuse. Discovery does not itself authorize invocation. User memories, traces, and vector indexes are not substitutes for a maintained capability inventory and its ownership metadata. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/agent-registry/overview"},{"id":"question-408","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team has installed Agents CLI but its coding assistant cannot find the bundled ADK development skills in a project workspace. They want project-scoped setup and to inspect the planned changes first. Which documented sequence fits?","answers":{"A":"Register the coding assistant's chat history in Memory Bank and restart the model endpoint.","B":"Preview agents-cli setup --workspace --dry-run, run project-scoped setup, and verify the skills are visible to the coding assistant.","C":"Deploy the production agent with --agent-identity and expect that deployment to install local coding skills.","D":"Upload the source repository to Vector Search so the CLI silently turns indexed files into executable skills."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agents CLI setup installs development skills and supports workspace scope and a dry-run preview. Verify discovery in the chosen coding assistant after setup. Runtime identity, conversational memory, and a vector index have different roles and do not install the CLI's local skill package. Reference: https://google.github.io/agents-cli/cli/"},{"id":"question-409","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A read-only tool receives HTTP 429 from an upstream API. The agent currently aborts the entire run or immediately retries forever. Which handling strategy best preserves useful recovery without worsening the overload?","answers":{"A":"Apply bounded retries and return a structured failure if the retry budget is exhausted.","B":"Return an empty success response so the model can continue without mentioning the upstream failure.","C":"Ask the model to retry until it succeeds without tracking attempts or an overall deadline.","D":"Restart the whole workflow after every 429, repeating all prior successful calls as well."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Recovery needs a retry budget and an explicit failure outcome. Respecting retry guidance and limits avoids unbounded traffic; structured results let orchestration choose fallback or explain the limitation. Empty success hides missing data, while workflow restarts or indefinite model retries can amplify load. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy"},{"id":"question-410","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A Python ADK agent must retrieve orders, loyalty tier, and open tickets before answering. The reads are independent. Although the tools are declared async, each calls a blocking HTTP library and observed latency remains additive. What should change?","answers":{"A":"Use nonblocking asynchronous I/O and bounded concurrent execution, then combine the required results.","B":"Keep blocking calls but put 'run in parallel' in each tool's description.","C":"Start concurrent reads and compose the answer after only the first result, ignoring the other required data.","D":"Increase output tokens so the model can perform more reasoning while the blocking requests run."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An async declaration alone does not make blocking I/O concurrent. Nonblocking clients and awaited concurrency allow independent reads to overlap, subject to deadlines and rate limits. Prompt descriptions do not alter execution, and answering from only one result violates the stated data requirement. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/performance/"},{"id":"question-411","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Two sub-agents in an ADK ParallelAgent both write their final result to state['analysis']. A downstream merger sometimes sees only one result. Each branch is otherwise independent. Which change most directly removes this write collision?","answers":{"A":"Increase the number of parallel branches so the missing result is likely to be regenerated.","B":"Use distinct branch output keys and merge explicitly after all branches finish.","C":"Keep the common key and instruct both models to finish at the same time.","D":"Read the common key repeatedly until its value happens to contain both complete results."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Independent outputs should have distinct state keys, followed by an explicit merge. Concurrent writes to one logical slot do not automatically become a combined result. More writers, model timing instructions, or repeated reads do not establish safe aggregation; truly shared mutable data needs coordination. Reference: https://adk.dev/agents/workflow-agents/parallel-agents/"},{"id":"question-412","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A RAG application must display a source link and section location for each supported answer claim. The retriever currently returns only passage text. What should the architecture preserve through retrieval, reranking, and generation?","answers":{"A":"Only each passage's relevance score, allowing the generator to reconstruct document URLs from memory.","B":"Passage identifiers and source metadata, with answer citations validated against the retrieved evidence.","C":"The model's self-reported confidence and a generic link to the company homepage.","D":"Every original document in full, replacing claim-level citations with an unlinked appendix."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Citations need a preserved mapping from evidence to an actual source and location. Verify that references correspond to supporting retrieved material; metadata alone does not guarantee claim support. Scores, guessed URLs, generic links, or a bulk appendix do not supply reliable claim-level provenance. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/answer"},{"id":"question-413","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A custom retrieval pipeline sends 800 passages to the Ranking API and requests topN=5. Latency remains high. The team assumes topN limits the passages the ranking service evaluates. What is the targeted correction?","answers":{"A":"Set topN=1 and retain the same 800 inputs because only returned records are scored.","B":"Reduce first-stage candidates based on measured recall, then rerank and select final context.","C":"Rerank the entire corpus nightly and reuse that ranking for every user query.","D":"Remove all first-stage filtering and rely on the reranker to perform document authorization."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"topN limits returned records; it does not mean only those records are ranked. A measured candidate budget reduces second-stage work while protecting recall. Relevance is query-dependent, and ranking is not document authorization. Candidate counts should be tuned for the workload rather than assumed universally optimal. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/ranking"},{"id":"question-414","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A policy assistant must answer only when retrieved evidence supports its claims. An evaluated retrieval fallback still finds no relevant material for some requests. What should the answer path do in those cases?","answers":{"A":"Explain that supporting information was not found and offer an appropriate clarification or escalation.","B":"Answer from model pretraining but attach the highest-scoring unrelated document as a citation.","C":"Lower the relevance threshold until at least one passage qualifies, regardless of measured precision.","D":"Treat a model confidence statement as evidence and suppress the retrieval failure from the response."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Insufficient evidence should lead to a transparent limitation or a useful next step under this source-constrained policy. Unrelated citations and self-reported confidence do not supply support. Relevance thresholds must be calibrated; lowering them merely to force an answer defeats the evidence requirement. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/answer"},{"id":"question-415","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A team wants to store JSON payloads and vectors together, retrieve and filter them in one managed system, and optionally use automatic embedding generation or bring its own embeddings. It prefers Collections of Data Objects over managing a standalone ANN index plus a separate payload store. Which service best matches?","answers":{"A":"Vector Search 1.0, treating vector IDs alone as the complete document-storage layer.","B":"Agent Retrieval, formerly Vector Search 2.0.","C":"Agent Registry, storing every document as an agent capability entry.","D":"Memory Bank, treating all enterprise documents as extracted user preferences."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agent Retrieval uses Collections and Data Objects for unified payload and vector storage and retrieval, with automatic embeddings or bring-your-own options. Vector Search 1.0 is the index-oriented alternative. Agent Registry and Memory Bank serve capability governance and conversational memory, respectively. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search-2/overview"},{"id":"question-416","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK agent generates a PDF report during a session. Later tools need to load a particular revision, and the UI must offer it for download. Which storage abstraction fits?","answers":{"A":"Encode the complete PDF in a state string and insert that value into every subsequent model prompt.","B":"Save it through the configured ArtifactService and retain a name/version reference in workflow state.","C":"Store the PDF only in a debug log so later tools can search logs for its binary bytes.","D":"Ask Memory Bank to extract the PDF into facts and discard the original file and revision information."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ADK artifacts represent named, versioned files or binary content through a configured service. A state reference can identify the required revision without embedding the whole file in conversation state. Logs or extracted memories do not preserve the requested file workflow; durable retention depends on the chosen artifact backend. Reference: https://adk.dev/artifacts/"},{"id":"question-417","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A voice agent must deliver audio incrementally and allow the user to interrupt while it is speaking. The current implementation waits for a complete text response, then generates audio. Which ADK interaction pattern best fits?","answers":{"A":"Use ADK live bidirectional streaming with audio and interruption event handling.","B":"Enable text-token SSE only and assume that this alone implements interruptible bidirectional audio.","C":"Increase the maximum answer length and play the complete audio after text generation finishes.","D":"Batch several users' utterances into one response so they share a single audio playback stream."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"ADK live agents support bidirectional interaction, including incremental media and interruption handling. One-way text-token streaming is useful for chat but does not by itself implement the full voice interaction. Longer buffered answers and cross-user batching do not meet the conversational requirement. Reference: https://adk.dev/live/"},{"id":"question-418","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A create_invoice tool times out after the server commits an invoice. The client cannot tell whether it succeeded and retries, sometimes creating duplicates. Which design makes retries safe for the same logical operation?","answers":{"A":"Generate a new request ID for each retry so every network attempt can be distinguished.","B":"Reuse an idempotency key, with atomic server-side deduplication and recorded results.","C":"Disable timeouts and assume a connection that remains open will always reveal the final outcome.","D":"Retry only once and rely on a low duplicate rate instead of maintaining operation identity."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An idempotency key must identify the logical operation across retries, and the server must enforce deduplication. Merely adding a field does not provide that behavior. New keys create new operations; indefinite connections and fewer retries do not resolve an uncertain committed result. Reference: https://google.aip.dev/155"},{"id":"question-419","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"One team wants managed enterprise search using supported source connectors and synchronized user access controls. Another has its own chunker, embedding pipeline, and payload store and needs detailed ANN index configuration. Which service mapping best fits?","answers":{"A":"Agent Search for the managed enterprise experience; Vector Search 1.0 for the custom index pipeline.","B":"Vector Search 1.0 for built-in source connectors; Agent Search as a standalone ANN index with no managed ingestion.","C":"Agent Registry for enterprise document ingestion; Memory Bank for custom corpus-wide ANN index tuning.","D":"Agent Retrieval for both, assuming that unified payload storage automatically imports every source's user ACLs."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Agent Search provides a managed search experience with supported integrations and configured data-source access control. Vector Search 1.0 fits the team that owns its retrieval pipeline and wants index control. Registries and memories have different roles; neither vector storage nor a product name automatically synchronizes external ACLs. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/data-source-access-control ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/configuring-indexes"},{"id":"question-420","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent writes useful advisory answers in many valid phrasings. Exact text matching rejects acceptable responses, and the team needs consistent scores for instruction adherence and completeness. Which scoring design fits best?","answers":{"A":"Use character equality after removing punctuation and require every answer to match a single reference.","B":"Use an expert-calibrated LLM judge with a rubric for the required answer qualities.","C":"Use response length as the primary score and penalize answers that are shorter than the reference.","D":"Use tool-call exact matching alone, because a correct tool sequence establishes final-answer completeness."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Rubric-based judging evaluates dimensions that surface-form comparison misses. Expert review checks whether scores reflect intended quality. Character matching and length confuse wording with usefulness, while trajectory correctness does not guarantee that the final answer follows instructions or includes required information. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluation-overview"},{"id":"question-421","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Two model versions must be compared on the same agent task. The team wants direct evidence of which produces better answers, while reducing input and presentation bias. Which design is best?","answers":{"A":"Generate paired outputs for the same representative inputs, blind model identities, vary presentation order, and aggregate judged preferences and ties.","B":"Compare the candidate on this month traffic with the baseline score from a different dataset collected last quarter.","C":"Generate outputs on disjoint inputs to maximize coverage, and compare raw average scores without accounting for input difficulty.","D":"Show the judge each model name and price, then ask it to infer which model should produce the stronger answer."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Paired inputs control task differences, while blinded and varied presentation reduces avoidable judge bias. The result remains an estimate that needs adequate coverage and calibrated judgment. Historical or disjoint datasets confound model effects with input difficulty; model metadata is not response-quality evidence. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/side-by-side-eval"},{"id":"question-422","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A candidate passes 9 of 10 sampled cases while the baseline passes 8 of the same 10. An engineer treats this as sufficient evidence to roll out a 10-percentage-point quality improvement. What is the strongest objection?","answers":{"A":"One extra pass is insufficient release evidence; analyze paired uncertainty on more representative cases.","B":"Ten examples invalidate any result, even a deterministic failure of a critical prohibited-action test.","C":"Repeat the same ten cases until a favorable aggregate appears, then use that run as the release evidence.","D":"Convert the change to a relative percentage; the numerical restatement removes the need to analyze uncertainty."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A small observed difference may reflect sampling or model variation rather than a durable gain. More representative paired evidence and uncertainty analysis support the decision. Small tests can still expose critical defects; selective reruns and percentage restatements do not improve statistical validity. Reference: https://firebase.google.com/docs/ab-testing/ab-concepts"},{"id":"question-423","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Mean agent latency is 900 ms, but an important minority of successful requests takes eight seconds. Which monitoring change most directly makes this problem visible?","answers":{"A":"Track the mean latency separately for each release and consider the service healthy while the mean remains under one second.","B":"Record latency distributions and p95/p99 by relevant request class, with separate visibility into errors and timeouts.","C":"Track the total number of successful requests, using increases in throughput as evidence of good responsiveness.","D":"Replace end-to-end latency with model-only latency to remove variation caused by tools and orchestration."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Percentiles expose slow tails hidden by a mean, and request-class slices identify affected workloads. Errors and timeouts need separate treatment to avoid misleading latency summaries. Throughput and model-only timings cannot describe the complete user-visible wait. Reference: https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"},{"id":"question-424","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A workflow spans a root agent, two subagents, a model endpoint, and remote tools. Logs exist, but the team cannot reliably reconstruct which child operations belong to one request. What should it implement?","answers":{"A":"Propagate trace context and create parent-child spans for model, tool, and subagent operations.","B":"A separate log file for every component, with timestamps but no shared request or trace identifiers.","C":"A dashboard of each service mean latency, treating the sum of those means as an individual request timeline.","D":"An extra model prompt asking it to reproduce its complete hidden reasoning after every production request."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Correlated spans connect operations and reveal timing and dependencies across components. Uncorrelated timestamps and aggregate means do not reconstruct individual causality. Tracing records instrumented events; it does not expose a model's complete private reasoning process. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/tracing"},{"id":"question-425","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A tool has occasional transient errors that the agent can recover from. The team wants actionable alerts when reliability threatens the user-facing service objective, rather than a page for each failed attempt. What should it configure?","answers":{"A":"Page on every tool error so the on-call engineer can decide whether each retry affects a user.","B":"Define a user-relevant reliability SLI and SLO, then alert on sustained error-budget burn with tool metrics for diagnosis.","C":"Ignore tool failures whenever the daily mean response latency remains below its normal threshold.","D":"Alert only when all tool calls fail; partial errors cannot materially affect a monthly availability target."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An SLO specifies acceptable user-visible reliability, and burn-rate alerts identify meaningful consumption of its error budget. Tool metrics explain causes. Paging on every recoverable failure creates noise, while average latency and total-outage-only rules miss significant partial degradation. Reference: https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"},{"id":"question-426","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A launch is expected to multiply agent traffic by twenty. Functional evals pass. Which test best reveals capacity risks before launch?","answers":{"A":"Run the golden dataset sequentially with model and tool calls mocked to ensure all expected answers still pass.","B":"Load-test realistic conversations and tool fan-out, measuring quotas, queueing, failures, and tails.","C":"Load-test only the HTTP frontend and extrapolate its request rate to the model and downstream tools.","D":"Increase maximum instances to the largest value and assume autoscaling will preserve the current latency."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Capacity testing must exercise the request mix and dependency demand that production creates. Token throughput, concurrency, tool fan-out, and downstream limits can constrain throughput before the frontend does. Sequential functional tests and a larger replica ceiling do not establish capacity under load. Reference: https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/optimize-and-scale"},{"id":"question-427","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A team has approved using the Preview Agent Runtime revision and traffic-management feature through v1beta1. It wants 5% of root-agent queries to reach a candidate and 95% to reach the current version. Which configuration fits?","answers":{"A":"Create active revisions and set root-agent manual routing to 5% candidate and 95% current.","B":"Keep traffic in Always latest mode and create the candidate revision; that mode initially limits new revisions to 5%.","C":"Point every client directly to the candidate revision resource and rely on the root-agent split to redistribute those requests.","D":"Overwrite an unversioned field on the agent and use that update as an isolated candidate revision for the test."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Preview supports manual percentage routing across active revisions. Always latest sends traffic to the newest revision; direct revision queries bypass root routing. Unversioned-field changes apply across revisions, so they do not create the isolated candidate described. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/manage-revisions-and-traffic"},{"id":"question-428","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A bad agent release is hard to roll back because the prior prompt and tool configuration were edited in place. Which release practice best makes recovery reproducible?","answers":{"A":"Version the agent bundle and its dependencies, retain a deployable known-good artifact, and rehearse rollback with state compatibility checks.","B":"Save only application source code, because the previous prompt can be regenerated from deployment timestamps.","C":"Keep a copy of the last prompt in an operations document and rebuild against whichever model and libraries are current.","D":"Record the previous average quality score and tune the live prompt until that score is reproduced."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Rollback requires a known configuration and deployable artifact, including prompts, tool settings, and model identifiers. Source alone or a prompt copy cannot reproduce changed dependencies. Recovery must also account for state compatibility; matching an old average score does not recreate an earlier release. Reference: https://sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering/"},{"id":"question-429","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent stores conversation state only in each serving process. Scale-in removes an instance, and a user next turn loses its history. Which architectural change addresses the root cause?","answers":{"A":"Use stable session identifiers with authoritative state in shared durable session storage.","B":"Enable session affinity while retaining process memory as the sole authoritative copy of the conversation.","C":"Increase the instance memory limit so more conversations fit before autoscaling terminates a process.","D":"Keep a single warm instance and use its local filesystem as the durable shared store for all future replicas."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"External durable session storage decouples conversation continuity from disposable serving instances. Agent Platform Sessions is one option; a suitable database can also work. Affinity, extra RAM, and a warm process do not preserve state when that process is replaced. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/sessions"},{"id":"question-430","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A Cloud Run tool service scales to zero between bursts. Startup spans account for its latency spikes. The team accepts some idle cost to improve typical responsiveness. Which change is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Configure a measured minimum-instance baseline and optimize startup, then verify burst tail latency and idle cost.","B":"Set one minimum instance and treat every future request as guaranteed free of cold-start latency.","C":"Increase the request timeout while keeping scale-to-zero and startup behavior unchanged.","D":"Increase only the maximum-instance ceiling; this guarantees new instances already exist when the next burst begins."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Minimum instances reduce startup exposure by retaining warm capacity, at a cost. They do not eliminate cold starts during larger bursts or replacement, and the minimum is a best-effort target. Timeouts change allowable waiting, while a maximum ceiling does not prewarm capacity. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/min-instances"},{"id":"question-431","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Finance wants cost per resolved support conversation. Some conversations fail after consuming substantial resources. Which definition best supports fair release comparisons?","answers":{"A":"Model input-token cost divided by all opened conversations, excluding output tokens and all failed attempts.","B":"Divide full attributable cohort spend by consistently defined resolved conversations, including failure costs.","C":"Total serving spend divided by the number of agent responses, counting retries and intermediate messages as successful outputs.","D":"Spend for successful conversations alone divided by those successes, omitting the resources spent on failures."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Unit cost should connect complete attributable spend with a stable business outcome. Including failed-attempt costs prevents apparently cheap releases from benefiting from many unresolved cases. Per-message or partial-token denominators measure different things and can hide inefficient orchestration. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/cost-optimization"},{"id":"question-432","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A RAG answer is fully supported by its retrieved document, but that document contains an obsolete policy. The answer receives a high groundedness score. What is the correct interpretation?","answers":{"A":"The judge must be broken, because groundedness proves whether a document is current and authoritative.","B":"Groundedness checks support from supplied evidence; assess source freshness and factual correctness separately.","C":"The answer is safe to approve because any response with a citation has independently verified its factual claims.","D":"Replace groundedness with final-response word overlap; lexical agreement can determine whether a source is obsolete."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Support by context and truth are different evaluation dimensions. A grounded answer can faithfully repeat outdated information. Source-quality and correctness checks address that gap; citations and lexical similarity alone do not establish current facts. Reference: https://adk.dev/evaluate/criteria/"},{"id":"question-433","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An A/B experiment compares end-to-end conversation resolution between two agent variants. The router chooses a variant independently on every turn, so conversations frequently switch variants. How should the experiment change?","answers":{"A":"Keep per-turn randomization and attribute the entire conversation outcome to whichever variant answered last.","B":"Assign and analyze a stable conversation-level variant, recording the assignment with each outcome.","C":"Use an exact 50/50 turn split; equal traffic percentages remove the effects of variant switching.","D":"Alternate variants on every turn deterministically so each conversation receives equal exposure to both."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For this conversation-level comparison, changing variants contaminates each outcome with both treatments. Stable assignment aligns exposure and measurement. Last-turn attribution, equal aggregate shares, and deterministic alternation do not isolate each variant's effect. Persistence across a broader user history may require a user-level unit. Reference: https://firebase.google.com/docs/ab-testing/ab-concepts"},{"id":"question-434","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A developer-written evalset scores 96%, but production review finds repeated failures on ambiguous multilingual requests and recovery after tool errors. What dataset improvement best addresses the observed gap?","answers":{"A":"Add more paraphrases of the highest-scoring single-turn English examples and retain the current intent distribution.","B":"Add reviewed cases stratified by real intents, languages, ambiguity, and tool-failure recovery, and report both slice and overall scores.","C":"Remove low-scoring production examples until the dataset has the same average score as the original developer set.","D":"Use a larger judge model on the unchanged dataset and infer that higher judge capacity solves missing coverage."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Evaluation must include the behaviors that fail in production. Representative strata and per-slice scores reveal gaps hidden by one aggregate. More of the same easy cases, removing failures, or changing the judge cannot provide evidence for scenarios absent from the dataset. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluation-dataset"},{"id":"question-435","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A team needs to catch regressions caused by code changes and also quality decay caused by changing external data when no commit occurs. Which evaluation schedule best covers both?","answers":{"A":"Combine CI release gates with recurring tests and sampled monitoring of the live agent.","B":"Run evaluation only on pull requests, because unchanged code implies that the validated agent behavior is unchanged.","C":"Evaluate the production model on a schedule but skip tools, retrieval, and session context to reduce evaluation cost.","D":"Use online user satisfaction as the sole release gate and stop running controlled regression cases."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Release tests cover candidate changes, while recurring and online evaluations cover the live system's evolving inputs. Both need relevant agent context. Model-only checks omit tools and retrieval, and satisfaction alone is a delayed, selective signal rather than controlled regression evidence. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/evaluate-offline; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/evaluate-online"},{"id":"question-436","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Trajectory failures show an agent selects search_orders instead of search_invoices. Both tool descriptions say only \"Search financial records,\" although the APIs have distinct functions. What is the best first targeted change?","answers":{"A":"Increase the model context window so both ambiguous descriptions fit more comfortably into the prompt.","B":"Clarify each tool purpose, when to use it, and parameter meaning, then rerun contrasting order and invoice cases.","C":"Rename the tools to shorter identifiers while preserving their descriptions and parameter definitions.","D":"Route every financial request to search_orders and ask the response judge to compensate for missing invoice results."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Tool descriptions and schemas help the model choose and invoke capabilities. Distinct use conditions address the observed ambiguity; contrasting tests check both routing paths. Extra context capacity, cosmetic renaming, and biased routing do not resolve overlapping semantics. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/"},{"id":"question-437","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A read-only external lookup intermittently hangs for 30 seconds. Users have an eight-second response budget. Which resilience policy best balances recovery and bounded latency?","answers":{"A":"Use a shorter tool deadline, bounded retries with jittered exponential backoff within the overall budget, and a clear degraded response when exhausted.","B":"Retry every timeout immediately until the tool succeeds, using the full 30-second deadline for each attempt.","C":"Set the tool deadline to eight seconds and automatically start three additional eight-second attempts after a timeout.","D":"Return an unverified cached answer for any user or request whenever the lookup is slow, regardless of freshness."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Deadlines and bounded backoff prevent transient faults from consuming an unbounded request budget or causing retry storms. These calls are read-only; mutations would also require idempotency safeguards. Repeated full deadlines exceed the budget, and fallback data must meet access and freshness requirements. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy; https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"},{"id":"question-438","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Long support conversations consume increasing tokens and begin losing important constraints. The team can identify durable task state and irrelevant older detail. Which context policy best balances continuity, cost, and quality?","answers":{"A":"Compact older history, preserve constraints and task state, and test long-session quality.","B":"Drop the oldest half of all messages whenever a token threshold is reached, regardless of their importance.","C":"Keep every historical message in every model request and select a larger context window whenever cost increases.","D":"Cache the full conversation and assume caching also removes irrelevant information from the model context."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Context compression can reduce repeated history, but summaries must preserve essential information and be evaluated. Arbitrary truncation can erase constraints; a larger window retains unnecessary cost and distraction. Caching optimizes reuse and does not itself select or summarize relevant state. Reference: https://adk.dev/context/compaction/"},{"id":"question-439","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"During a burst, the agent runtime has spare CPU, but model calls return HTTP 429 under a pay-as-you-go serving arrangement. Which response best targets the observed bottleneck?","answers":{"A":"Increase runtime replicas and allow each one to retry failed model requests immediately.","B":"Confirm the model capacity or quota condition, smooth demand, use bounded backoff, and evaluate the appropriate quota or throughput option.","C":"Disable tool tracing so the model endpoint receives fewer telemetry records in each inference request.","D":"Increase model output-token limits so fewer 429 responses are returned while processing the same request rate."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A model-side 429 can reflect capacity or applicable quota limits. Inspect the serving arrangement rather than assuming every limit is adjustable. Demand smoothing, bounded retries, and a suitable capacity option address pressure; more callers and immediate retries can amplify it. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/deploy/error-code-429"},{"id":"question-440","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A deployed ADK agent emits events before its final response, but the frontend buffers the entire HTTP result. Users see no progress for twelve seconds. Tool execution still takes most of that time. Which change best improves perceived responsiveness?","answers":{"A":"Move from a managed runtime to GKE, because Agent Runtime cannot stream agent responses.","B":"Consume the response stream; measure first useful output separately from workflow completion.","C":"Enable response streaming and declare the twelve-second tool workflow complete as soon as the first event arrives.","D":"Cache the final answer from another conversation and display it while the current workflow performs user-specific actions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agent Runtime supports ADK response streaming. A streaming client can expose useful events sooner, but it does not remove tool latency or guarantee token-by-token output for every configuration. Total completion and error handling still matter; another user's answer is not valid progress. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/use-an-adk-agent"},{"id":"question-441","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Before replacing a transaction-capable agent, the team wants to compare candidate behavior on copies of live inputs without delivering candidate answers or executing duplicate customer actions. Which design fits?","answers":{"A":"Shadow inputs with isolated session state and sandboxed mutations; discard candidate output.","B":"Use shadow traffic against the same production mutation tools; discarding responses prevents any customer-visible effects.","C":"Use a 5% canary with real mutation tools and count those users as having no exposure because most traffic is unchanged.","D":"Deploy a standby version and send it no requests until a full cutover, then compare its first production outcomes retrospectively."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Shadowing copies inputs while the established version serves users. For an agent, suppressing final text does not suppress tool side effects: state and mutations must be isolated. Shadow tests still consume resources and may differ from live execution; canaries expose users and idle standbys generate no comparison evidence. Reference: https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"},{"id":"question-442","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A candidate improves judged quality by four percentage points but triples p95 latency and doubles cost per resolved conversation. It violates the agreed latency SLO. What should the release decision do?","answers":{"A":"Promote because any measured quality gain is sufficient to justify additional resource consumption.","B":"Hold broad release; test optimizations or routing against the joint quality, cost, and latency criteria.","C":"Average quality, latency, and cost as raw numbers so the single largest number determines the winning version.","D":"Reject all future quality improvements from this candidate model, even if routing can satisfy the latency and cost constraints."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Quality, responsiveness, and cost express different business constraints. A violated agreed SLO should not be silently overridden by another score. Targeted routing or optimization may preserve benefits; arbitrary numeric averaging and permanent rejection both avoid evaluating the actual trade-off. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/performance-optimization; https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/ai-ml/cost-optimization"},{"id":"question-443","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An agent serves EU and APAC customers. EU requests must remain within the EU for processing and storage, while APAC latency is poor through the current EU deployment. Which architecture best addresses both requirements?","answers":{"A":"Place the runtime in an EU region but use global model endpoints and worldwide failover for all requests.","B":"Use geography-aware deployments and routing, verifying each model processing commitment and keeping EU data, sessions, tools, and failover within the allowed boundary.","C":"Keep only the primary database in the EU; runtime, model inference, trace payloads, and fallback endpoints need no placement review.","D":"Use one global response cache for user-specific outputs so every region can reuse the nearest existing answer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Placement must cover the full request path. An EU runtime alone does not constrain a globally routed model or external tool. Check supported model and endpoint processing commitments, storage, telemetry, and failover. Region names should not be assumed to promise narrower processing locality than the documentation provides. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/resources/data-residency"},{"id":"question-444","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A production incident issued incorrect refunds. Service is restored. Which postmortem outcome most improves future reliability?","answers":{"A":"Document the responsible developer and require that person to approve every future prompt edit personally.","B":"Assign owners to verified fixes, regression cases, execution safeguards, and improved detection.","C":"Record the timeline and close the incident once the on-call rotation confirms the dashboards are green.","D":"Raise the alert threshold to avoid repeating the page volume seen during the incident and retain the current tests."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A useful postmortem converts evidence into owned, verifiable improvements. Agent incidents should feed regression coverage and execution safeguards as appropriate. Personal blame does not address system causes; a timeline alone lacks corrective action, and suppressing alerts can hide recurrence. Reference: https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"id":"question-445","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Two retrievers return different document rankings for the same RAG queries. The team wants to measure how many relevant documents are found and whether the best evidence is ranked early, independently of answer generation. Which evaluation fits?","answers":{"A":"Score generated answers for ROUGE and use response length to infer retrieval coverage and ranking quality.","B":"Use reviewed query-document relevance labels to compare recall at k and a ranking metric such as NDCG at k.","C":"Compare mean vector-similarity scores directly across embedding models and choose the retriever with the larger numbers.","D":"Measure corpus document count and model groundedness, treating these as direct measures of ranking effectiveness."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Recall at k measures recovery of labeled relevant documents; NDCG accounts for where relevant results appear. Generation scores mix retrieval and synthesis, while raw similarities can be incomparable across embedding models. Corpus size and groundedness do not establish ranking quality. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/evaluate-search-quality"},{"id":"question-446","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An agent's previous attempt to 'improve the orders API' changed its response schema and missed the actual latency goal. Which revised task definition best supports a reviewable implementation plan?","answers":{"A":"Reduce the number of functions in the orders module and let the agent decide which response fields customers probably still need.","B":"Under the supplied load test, reduce p95 GET /orders latency below 200 ms, preserve the response schema, and pass contract tests.","C":"Modernize GET /orders using the latest framework conventions and provide a detailed explanation of why the result should perform better.","D":"Optimize GET /orders for the fastest possible response, allowing the agent to replace contract tests when they constrain the redesign."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Measurable acceptance criteria and explicit invariants make scope and verification reviewable. Function counts are a proxy, modernization leaves the goal undefined, and replacing contract tests relaxes the required compatibility constraint. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/implementation-plan/"},{"id":"question-447","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A coding agent fixing one monorepo module repeatedly edits unrelated services after receiving a huge repository dump. The module has dependencies outside its directory. What context strategy is most appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Load every repository file again in a fresh session, placing the target module last so its content is most recent.","B":"Supply the failing case, relevant entry points and interface constraints, then let the agent search dependencies as needed.","C":"Restrict the agent to the target directory and prohibit inspecting callers, shared schemas, build files, or dependency implementations.","D":"Provide only the failing line and ask the agent to infer the surrounding architecture from language and framework conventions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Focused evidence narrows exploration while allowing discovery of necessary dependencies. A bulk dump adds noise, directory-only isolation hides contracts, and a single line omits essential context. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/best-practices/"},{"id":"question-448","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A reproducible bug causes an API to mishandle expired sessions. The team wants an agent-generated fix with durable evidence against recurrence. Which workflow best meets that goal?","answers":{"A":"Add a test that fails for the reported behavior, fix the implementation, and rerun that test plus related session-management tests.","B":"Patch the suspected condition, then update test expectations to match the new behavior before running the suite and creating the PR.","C":"Ask a second agent to inspect the diff and accept the first agent's claim that tests passed without retaining the actual results.","D":"Add extensive debug logging around session validation, then wait for production traffic to demonstrate whether the bug persists."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A failing reproducer links the change to the defect, and related tests check regressions. Matching tests to the new implementation can bless a mistake; review alone does not prove execution; production observation postpones verification. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/best-practices/"},{"id":"question-449","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A company's policy allows approved low-risk maintenance PRs to merge after CI, but authentication and IAM changes require a security owner's approval. Agents can edit source code but must not bypass this policy. Which control design fits?","answers":{"A":"Classify changes using the agent's final summary and permit it to merge whenever it reports that no sensitive files were touched.","B":"Enforce path-based ownership review and protected-branch checks in the repository, alongside the existing CI gates.","C":"Give each agent a merge token and place the sensitive-path approval requirement in an Always On workspace rule.","D":"Require a second coding agent to approve sensitive changes and treat that automated review as the security owner's approval."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Repository-enforced gates apply independently of the agent's description or compliance with prompts. A merge-capable token plus a rule is weaker enforcement, and another model does not satisfy the stated human-owner requirement. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/overview"},{"id":"question-450","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity deployment tool accepts a destination environment. A maintained policy script must inspect that argument and block prohibited destinations before the tool runs. Which customization is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"A PostToolUse hook that records the destination and sends the agent a warning after the deployment finishes.","B":"A PreToolUse hook matching the deployment tool and returning a deny decision when the destination violates policy.","C":"An Always On rule that lists permitted environments and asks the agent to double-check its destination before deployment.","D":"A Stop hook that reviews the transcript and deletes the generated deployment summary whenever it finds a prohibited destination."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"PreToolUse can inspect a proposed call and deny execution. PostToolUse and Stop run too late to prevent the action, while a prose rule guides behavior. The gate must cover the actual tool route and be protected from unauthorized modification. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/hooks/"},{"id":"question-451","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Developers' personal Antigravity rules conflict with a repository's formatting conventions. The team needs consistent checked-in output across contributors without depending on undocumented prompt-precedence behavior. What should it do?","answers":{"A":"Version the workspace guidance, remove or reconcile conflicting personal instructions, and enforce formatting with the repository's formatter and CI.","B":"Assume workspace rules universally override global rules and let the model decide which formatter to run when instructions conflict.","C":"Move all conventions into each developer's global file and update those files manually whenever repository standards change.","D":"Duplicate the repository convention in several Always On rules so repetition causes it to outrank personal instructions."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Shared guidance plus deterministic formatting checks produces reproducible output. Global/workspace rules should not be treated as a universal conflict-resolution guarantee; manual global copies drift, and repeated instructions do not establish a reliable precedence mechanism. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/rules-workflows/"},{"id":"question-452","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity research subagent investigates a large dependency while the parent prepares code changes. A reviewer assumes separate context automatically prevents the subagent from modifying the parent's files or using its permissions. Which correction is accurate?","answers":{"A":"Subagents share one conversation window, so the only effective separation is to run them with separate user accounts.","B":"Context separation limits conversation clutter; workspace mode and inherited permissions determine access, so filesystem and security isolation need explicit design.","C":"Subagents always use separate credentials and read-only filesystems, even when they inherit the parent's workspace configuration.","D":"A subagent's final summary is the only information any other agent can access, which makes workspace permissions irrelevant."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Antigravity distinguishes context isolation, workspace options, and inherited safety settings. Separate context is not an OS or credential boundary. Shared workspaces and permissions require deliberate control, and agent communication is not limited universally to one final summary. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/subagents/"},{"id":"question-453","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Six teams need to install the same Antigravity migration skills, coding rules, MCP definitions, and event hooks as one maintained customization bundle. Which packaging mechanism best fits?","answers":{"A":"An IDE extension containing only editor shortcuts and themes, distributed separately from all agent-readable customization files.","B":"A namespaced Antigravity plugin with a manifest and the supported customization components.","C":"A single Always On rule containing the full source of each hook, all MCP definitions, and every migration procedure.","D":"A task-specific subagent prompt copied into each team's chat history before its first migration."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Plugins bundle multiple customization types for installation and reuse. Editor UI integration alone does not package the described agent assets, a rule cannot substitute for executable/configuration components, and copied prompts lack a maintained installation unit. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/plugins/"},{"id":"question-454","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity agent changes a frontend form to fix a keyboard-navigation bug. The defect involves focus movement after a validation error. What should the reviewer request as direct behavioral evidence?","answers":{"A":"A successful TypeScript build and a screenshot of the initial form before any keyboard interaction occurs.","B":"A browser run of the original keyboard steps after the fix, with assertions on focus and validation behavior and a recorded walkthrough.","C":"A second model's review of the changed event handlers, with a confidence score indicating that browser verification is unnecessary.","D":"A snapshot of the form's static HTML, compared with the previous snapshot to show that the input elements still exist."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Interactive browser verification can exercise the focus sequence and record observable results. Type checking, static snapshots, and model review cannot establish the required runtime behavior by themselves. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/ide/browser/"},{"id":"question-455","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A frontend agent invents component props because its training examples differ from the internal design-system version in the repository. The system has a versioned schema service. Which integration best addresses the problem?","answers":{"A":"Expose version-specific schemas and examples through an MCP query tool, then validate generated usage with the project's compiler and tests.","B":"Expose only the newest schema through MCP and have the agent migrate every affected component to that version during the bug fix.","C":"Increase the context window and include examples from public component libraries whose APIs resemble the internal design system.","D":"Add a skill instructing the agent to use conventional prop names while keeping the internal schema service inaccessible."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Version-matched tool grounding supplies the actual contract, and compilation/tests validate use. Newest-version data can mismatch the repository; public examples and generic naming guidance remain guesses. MCP access improves grounding without guaranteeing correct generated code. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/"},{"id":"question-456","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"Integration tests run by a coding agent need one test-database credential from Google Secret Manager. The test harness can call Google APIs directly. Which design minimizes unnecessary secret exposure?","answers":{"A":"Store an encrypted copy of the password in the repository and give every agent the common decryption key.","B":"Have the test process fetch only the required secret at runtime using its scoped workload identity, without printing it or adding it to prompts.","C":"Grant project-wide Secret Manager access and export all test secrets into the coding agent's environment for convenience.","D":"Write the password into a temporary workspace rules file, then ask the agent to delete that file before creating the PR."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Secret-level access and direct API retrieval limit credential distribution. Broad environment export increases exposure, repository encryption still requires key handling, and temporary prompt files can persist in context or artifacts. Retrieved secrets must also be kept out of logs. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/best-practices"},{"id":"question-457","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A multi-day Antigravity refactor affects a public API and a database migration. The sponsor requires approval of scope and rollback strategy before edits begin. How should the workflow be configured?","answers":{"A":"Use Always Proceed for artifact review and ask the agent to summarize the plan after it has implemented the changes.","B":"Require review of an implementation-plan artifact covering scope, interfaces, migration, rollback, and verification before authorizing execution.","C":"Approve the final commit message in advance and let the agent decide scope and rollback details during execution.","D":"Ask for an implementation plan but configure automatic continuation so the agent can start editing while reviewers read it."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An explicit review checkpoint makes the requested pre-edit decision possible. Always Proceed, retrospective summaries, and automatic continuation do not satisfy it; a commit message lacks the required design detail. Plan review is configurable rather than universally automatic. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/implementation-plan/"},{"id":"question-458","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"CI coding-agent sandboxes currently have unrestricted internet egress. Builds require approved packages and a model endpoint but no arbitrary uploads. What hardening best preserves those needs?","answers":{"A":"Allow outbound TCP 443 to any host and rely on TLS to prevent source code from leaving the organization.","B":"Default-deny egress and explicitly permit the required endpoints, preferably through controlled package mirrors and an egress proxy.","C":"Allow every public package-registry domain and conclude that source exfiltration is impossible because registries serve software downloads.","D":"Route outbound traffic through a NAT gateway while retaining unrestricted destination access for builds and model calls."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Enforced egress rules reduce destinations while preserving necessary access. TLS does not restrict destination or content, registry allowlisting is not an absolute exfiltration guarantee, and a NAT gateway alone does not enforce destination restrictions. Allowed destinations and upload capabilities still need review. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/network-policy"},{"id":"question-459","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An agent must refactor a legacy billing module whose observable behavior is undocumented and untested. Public behavior must remain compatible except for separately approved defects. What should happen before structural rewrites?","answers":{"A":"Generate the cleaner implementation first, then ask the agent to derive a regression suite exclusively from the replacement code.","B":"Identify observable contracts and create reviewed characterization tests for representative cases before changing the structure.","C":"Adopt the newest framework defaults as the expected behavior and use compilation success as the compatibility criterion.","D":"Run a coverage tool on the old module and treat its report as a complete specification of behavior that consumers depend on."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Characterization tests create an executable baseline that reviewers can compare with intended contracts. Tests from replacement code may encode regressions, framework defaults can change behavior, and coverage measures execution rather than specifying correctness. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/best-practices/"},{"id":"question-460","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An enterprise has dozens of lengthy coding procedures, but most tasks need only one or two. Loading every procedure as an Always On rule is crowding out task context. Which Antigravity customization strategy fits best?","answers":{"A":"Compress every procedure into one global rule, sacrificing examples and edge cases so all procedures fit into every session.","B":"Create focused skills with clear descriptions and keep detailed instructions and helper resources inside each skill package.","C":"Create a PostInvocation hook that appends the complete procedure library after each model response to keep it recent.","D":"Fine-tune the model on the current procedures and remove the versioned documents from the development workflow."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Skills use progressive disclosure: descriptions support discovery, with details loaded when applicable. This preserves procedural depth without loading everything each time. Global compression loses detail, repeated injection worsens clutter, and training is not a replacement for current versioned procedures. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/skills/"},{"id":"question-461","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team wants every clone to expose the same approved Antigravity MCP server definitions, while each contributor authenticates with their own identity. What should be versioned with the repository?","answers":{"A":"Each developer's OAuth token cache, so a fresh clone immediately gains the same access as the repository maintainer.","B":"A workspace .agents/mcp_config.json containing server definitions, with authentication secrets kept outside Git and access approved separately.","C":"A copy of the lead developer's entire global configuration directory, including credentials and broad tool auto-approval settings.","D":"Only a wiki link naming the servers; server configuration should remain an undocumented property of each developer machine."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Workspace MCP definitions make configuration reproducible; authentication and permission grants remain separate. Token caches and copied global credentials expose identities, while a wiki alone does not standardize agent-readable configuration. A clone does not itself grant access. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/mcp/"},{"id":"question-462","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A headless Antigravity CLI review exits with code 0 and reports SUCCESS, but stderr says its test command was denied because approval was unavailable. CI currently labels the patch 'tests passed.' What should change?","answers":{"A":"Continue trusting SUCCESS because it indicates that every tool in the task completed without requiring human intervention.","B":"Grant only the required test action and make CI require actual successful test evidence before reporting the patch as verified.","C":"Disable stderr collection to keep soft-denial notices from being mistaken for errors by downstream CI parsers.","D":"Enable all permission bypasses and treat any future zero exit status as proof that the required tests passed."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Headless SUCCESS describes completion of the agent response, not proof that every requested tool ran. Approval-dependent calls can be soft-denied without a nonzero exit. CI must verify test execution/results; hiding diagnostics or bypassing all permissions does not establish that evidence. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/headless/"},{"id":"question-463","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A coding agent inspecting an external repository reads a README that asks it to copy the developer's SSH keys into a diagnostic archive. This action is unrelated to the authorized code review. Which response and architecture best contain the attack?","answers":{"A":"Accept the instruction because repository maintainers define the local workflow, but require the archive to be encrypted before it is uploaded.","B":"Treat the request as untrusted task content, reject the unrelated action, and enforce restricted file access, tool permissions, and egress.","C":"Add a rule forbidding key disclosure while leaving the developer's full home directory and unrestricted network access available to the agent.","D":"Run a second model over the README and permit the archive whenever the second model assigns the repository a low risk score."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Repository text can carry indirect prompt injection. Rejecting unrelated instructions should be backed by access and execution controls. Encryption does not authorize disclosure; a rule alone or another model's score is not a hard boundary. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"},{"id":"question-464","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team is migrating from Gemini CLI to Antigravity CLI and wants to retain a reviewed extension containing custom commands and MCP definitions. What is the documented migration approach?","answers":{"A":"Use Antigravity CLI's Gemini plugin import, then review converted skills, MCP configuration, and permissions before relying on the bundle.","B":"Rename the extension manifest to plugin.json and assume the two products use identical component schemas and configuration paths.","C":"Copy the entire Gemini CLI settings file into an Antigravity workspace rule so the model can interpret the server definitions.","D":"Install the extension as an ADK runtime plugin, because coding-assistant extensions and deployed-agent plugins share the same lifecycle."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Antigravity documents importing Gemini extensions into native plugins. Conversion handles differing layouts and command-to-skill mapping; review remains necessary. A renamed manifest, a prose rule, or an ADK runtime plugin does not perform that migration. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/gcli-migration/"},{"id":"question-465","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity coding agent repeats small dependency changes after the same deterministic build failure, without collecting new evidence. The task has a fixed time budget. What intervention is most effective?","answers":{"A":"Increase the retry budget and instruct the agent to avoid interrupting the current strategy until one attempt succeeds.","B":"Interrupt the loop, inspect the first failure and environment assumptions, then resume with a corrected hypothesis and bounded verification steps.","C":"Move the same full conversation to a larger-context model and request the identical retry sequence with more detailed explanations.","D":"Mark the task complete once the dependency file changes substantially, leaving the unresolved build for the integration team."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Repeated attempts without new evidence warrant course correction. Diagnosing the failure and supplying missing constraints changes the search; extra retries or a larger context do not fix the underlying assumption. Code churn is not task completion. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/cli/prompting/"},{"id":"question-466","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An external tool requires an API key. The agent uses Agent Identity auth manager, and only the tool integration needs the key. Which configuration best reduces unnecessary credential exposure?","answers":{"A":"Put the API key in a tool description so the model can include it whenever the tool is selected.","B":"Use an IAM-restricted API-key auth provider with the authenticated tool integration.","C":"Store the key in Agent Registry metadata so every agent that discovers the tool can retrieve it.","D":"Put an encrypted key and its decryption key together in the system prompt to simplify rotation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An API-key auth provider manages credentials separately from model instructions, and IAM controls access. Tool descriptions, discoverable registry metadata, and prompts unnecessarily distribute secrets. Encrypting a key alongside its decryption key does not establish an access boundary. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/auth-with-api-key-v2"},{"id":"question-467","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"Several Agent Runtime agents share a downloaded service account key. Security wants independently attributable identities and stronger protection against replay of stolen Google Cloud access tokens. Which migration best addresses both goals?","answers":{"A":"Give each agent a copy of the same key with a different filename and include the filename in logs.","B":"Enable Agent Identity for each agent and grant least-privilege IAM access, using its managed certificate-bound Google Cloud credentials.","C":"Keep the shared key but shorten the prompts so fewer credentials appear in model context.","D":"Register distinct display names while continuing to authenticate all requests with the shared key."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agent Identity supplies per-agent identities and binds Google Cloud access tokens to managed X.509 credentials. This is stronger than merely asserting that all short-lived workload tokens are non-exportable. Renaming keys, reducing prompt length, or changing display names leaves the shared authentication authority unchanged. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/agent-identity-overview"},{"id":"question-468","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A third-party MCP server labels its SQL tool read-only, but its implementation accepts arbitrary SQL using a database administrator credential. An analytics agent must have strictly read-only access. What is the strongest correction?","answers":{"A":"Trust the read-only annotation because MCP clients are required to treat every server annotation as authoritative.","B":"Constrain the exposed capability and enforce validation plus read-only database permissions.","C":"Remove UPDATE and DELETE examples from the tool description while retaining the administrator credential.","D":"Let the model classify each generated SQL statement as safe and bypass database authorization after approval."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Tool metadata is not an authorization boundary. MCP treats annotations as untrusted unless supplied by trusted servers and requires server access controls and input validation. Database permissions should independently enforce read-only access. Description changes or model self-review leave the powerful credential usable. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools"},{"id":"question-469","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"Investigators must determine which Google Cloud objects an agent read or modified. The application currently stores sampled Cloud Trace spans but has not reviewed service audit-log coverage. Which preparation best supports future investigations?","answers":{"A":"Verify relevant service audit coverage, enable required Data Access logs before incidents, and retain them with Admin Activity logs under controlled access.","B":"Increase trace sampling alone, because every span necessarily contains the authoritative downstream authorization decision.","C":"Enable Admin Activity logging alone, because it records all reads of user-provided data.","D":"Enable Data Access logs after an incident and request the service to regenerate the preceding month of missing records."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Audit logs record supported service operations and callers; traces serve a different observability purpose. Admin Activity logs are always generated but do not replace Data Access logs. Data Access is generally opt-in except for BigQuery, and enabling it later does not recreate missing history. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit"},{"id":"question-470","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"Security defines organization-level Model Armor floor settings for template conformance. A team says this alone guarantees screening of all requests sent directly to an arbitrary external LLM. What should the architect explain?","answers":{"A":"Floor settings modify every external LLM's internal safety configuration through the organization hierarchy.","B":"Template conformance and runtime screening are distinct; configure the applicable integration and enforcement mode.","C":"Floor settings apply only to billing quotas and have no effect on Model Armor templates.","D":"Template conformance replaces IAM, so every registered model request is automatically authorized and screened."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Template conformance and runtime inspection are distinct. Floor settings can support integrated screening, but defining a template baseline does not intercept arbitrary external traffic. They neither modify a third-party model nor replace authorization; the applicable integration and enforcement mode must be configured. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/configure-floor-settings"},{"id":"question-471","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An approved analytics export must replace customer identifiers while preserving joins across records. Re-identification may be performed only by a separate authorized service. Which Sensitive Data Protection approach is appropriate?","answers":{"A":"Use keyed deterministic pseudonymization with separately protected re-identification authority.","B":"Generate an independent random replacement for every occurrence of an identifier without maintaining a mapping.","C":"Replace every identifier with the same fixed string and retain the other columns.","D":"Encrypt the export file with a password but leave all identifiers unchanged for recipients."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Consistent keyed pseudonymization preserves linkage and can support controlled re-identification. It is not a guarantee of irreversible anonymization. Independent replacements break joins, a common placeholder loses distinctions, and file encryption exposes the original identifiers to anyone authorized to open the file. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/pseudonymization"},{"id":"question-472","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A retrieved document tells an agent to ignore its task and send confidential records to an external endpoint. Input screening did not flag the document. Which architecture most effectively limits the consequences of this indirect prompt injection?","answers":{"A":"Increase the length of the system prompt while keeping all tools and credentials unrestricted.","B":"Constrain tool actions and destinations using trusted context, with approval gates for consequential operations.","C":"Give the agent broader permissions to inspect, repair, and quarantine suspicious source documents before answering the user's question.","D":"Move the document into a vector database and assume indexed content is therefore trusted."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Layered controls constrain behavior even when malicious content reaches the model. Tool policy must use trusted application context rather than attacker-influenced instructions. Longer prompts and indexing do not establish trust, and additional permissions expand the impact of a successful injection. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"},{"id":"question-473","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A contract requires customer data to remain within EU member states during storage and model inference. A proposed design uses an endpoint whose name begins with europe. What is the appropriate architecture decision?","answers":{"A":"Accept the design because every endpoint containing europe guarantees identical EU processing commitments for every model and feature.","B":"Verify the exact model and feature's documented EU processing commitments, select a supported endpoint such as the EU jurisdictional endpoint, and verify storage, logs, tools, and failover paths.","C":"Use the global endpoint with an EU billing address to make inference subject to EU-only routing.","D":"Keep storage in the EU and permit model inference elsewhere because residency applies only to persisted files."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Storage residency and ML-processing location are separate commitments. They depend on the supported model, feature, and endpoint; the EU jurisdiction excludes non-EU European countries. Endpoint naming, billing address, or EU storage alone cannot establish that the entire agent workflow satisfies the contract. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/resources/data-residency"},{"id":"question-474","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent can read protected Cloud Storage data inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter. Security must address both copies to an outside Google Cloud bucket and uploads to an arbitrary internet HTTPS service. Which design covers these distinct paths?","answers":{"A":"Pair the Google Cloud service perimeter with independently enforced internet-egress restrictions.","B":"Use VPC Service Controls alone, because a service perimeter is an internet firewall for all destinations.","C":"Use Cloud NAT alone, because translation permits outbound traffic only to organization-owned endpoints.","D":"Apply a storage IAM allow role alone, because it also constrains where already-read bytes can be sent."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"VPC Service Controls can constrain protected Google Cloud service operations across a perimeter but does not block arbitrary third-party internet APIs. Internet egress needs a separate enforced control. NAT supplies connectivity, and IAM read permission does not govern every later use of downloaded data. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls/docs/overview"},{"id":"question-475","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"Before release, an agent passes ordinary task-success tests. Security wants evidence that guardrails withstand malicious retrieval content, unauthorized tool requests, and attempts to cross tenant boundaries. Which evaluation plan is most useful?","answers":{"A":"Approve release because a high average task-success rate implies low security risk.","B":"Test adversarial end-to-end outcomes and preserve discovered bypasses as regression cases.","C":"Ask the production model whether its own system prompt is secure and accept its confidence score.","D":"Test only the final response for offensive language while allowing all tool actions during evaluation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Security evaluations should exercise failure paths and inspect actual actions, not just helpful final responses. Adversarial regression cases provide repeatable evidence about guardrails, although passing them cannot prove absence of all attacks. Ordinary task metrics and model self-assessment do not measure unauthorized effects. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"},{"id":"question-476","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An internal A2A service publishes authentication requirements in its Agent Card, but its task handler accepts requests without validating credentials. What must change before production use?","answers":{"A":"Move the Agent Card to a private repository; publishing requirements is sufficient once discovery is restricted.","B":"Authenticate encrypted requests and authorize each operation against the verified caller's permissions.","C":"Accept the caller identity from a natural-language field in each task because A2A establishes trust between agents.","D":"Require a valid TLS server certificate and skip caller authorization for traffic from the internal network."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Agent Card advertises requirements; the server must enforce them. A2A uses established transport authentication and implementation-specific authorization. Restricted discovery, caller-provided text, and server TLS authentication do not establish that a particular client may perform a requested action. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/"},{"id":"question-477","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"To investigate Model Armor detections, a team enables content logging and grants all developers access to the log destination. Conversations can contain customer secrets. Which governance response best preserves useful evidence while reducing exposure?","answers":{"A":"Minimize sensitive log content and enforce justified access and retention policies.","B":"Treat Model Armor screening as proof that every logged prompt is safe to share with all developers.","C":"Assume Model Armor's stateless processing means content cannot be retained anywhere after logging is enabled.","D":"Retain complete conversations indefinitely because security logs are exempt from data-governance policies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Model Armor does not durably retain analyzed content during ordinary processing, but explicitly enabled logging can retain content according to the logging configuration. Screening is not permission to share logs broadly. Data minimization, access control, and retention must cover this additional data store. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/overview"},{"id":"question-478","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A reviewer approves an agent's proposed payment of EUR 12,000 to vendor X. Before execution, a retry produces EUR 18,000 to vendor Y, but the workflow reuses the earlier approval. Which design closes this gap?","answers":{"A":"Treat approval as permission for any payment generated in the same conversation.","B":"Bind durable, authenticated approval to exact transaction details and invalidate it after changes.","C":"Ask the model whether the changed payment has equivalent intent and reuse approval if it agrees.","D":"Store only the reviewer's display name and an approved boolean that every retry can reuse."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Human confirmation must authorize the action that actually executes. Binding the decision to the transaction and retaining reviewer evidence prevents a changed request from borrowing prior approval. Conversation-wide flags, display names, and model judgments do not establish approval of the new payment. Reference: https://adk.dev/tools-custom/confirmation/"},{"id":"question-479","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A payment-preparation agent can also invoke the approval tool using the same identity. Finance requires independent approval. Which redesign provides an enforceable separation of duties?","answers":{"A":"Create a second model instance for approval but let both instances share the original credential and permissions.","B":"Enforce separate preparation and approval principals, preventing access to each other's authority.","C":"Rename the two tools prepare and approve while keeping their authorization policy identical.","D":"Add a second log entry before payment execution while retaining one principal for both steps."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Separate trust boundaries need independently restricted authority. Backend enforcement must prevent the preparation principal from satisfying approval through the same or obtainable credentials. Separate model instances, tool names, or logs do not create independent control when their effective permissions remain shared. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely"},{"id":"question-480","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A custom MCP proxy accepts an access token issued only for a downstream SaaS API, skips audience validation, and forwards that token to the SaaS API. The team calls this identity propagation. Which correction follows MCP security guidance?","answers":{"A":"Keep the design and log the token so investigators can identify the original caller.","B":"Require a token intended for the MCP server, validate it, and establish a separate appropriately authorized credential for downstream access.","C":"Accept any signed token whose expiration is in the future, regardless of its intended resource.","D":"Forward the token only over TLS, which removes the need to validate its audience or authorization."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Unvalidated token passthrough crosses authorization boundaries and is forbidden by MCP guidance. The proxy must authenticate its own caller with an appropriately targeted token and manage downstream authorization separately. Signature checks and TLS do not correct an audience mismatch, and logging secrets creates additional exposure. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2025-11-25/tutorials/security/security_best_practices"},{"id":"question-481","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent stores records in a CMEK-enabled service. The team claims that using a customer-managed key also prevents an authorized agent from sending plaintext records to an unapproved model. Which assessment is correct?","answers":{"A":"CMEK controls all subsequent network transfers of data originally encrypted with that key.","B":"CMEK governs supported encryption at rest; plaintext use needs independent authorization and egress controls.","C":"CMEK removes the need for IAM on the data service because only callers with direct KMS permissions can read data.","D":"Rotating the CMEK forces every downstream model to erase previously received plaintext."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CMEK provides key control for supported encryption-at-rest integrations. An authorized service can decrypt data for legitimate reads, so subsequent plaintext use requires other controls. Data access still depends on IAM, and key rotation neither governs network destinations nor recalls copies already sent elsewhere. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kms/docs/cmek"},{"id":"question-482","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An incident runbook promises that removing an agent's IAM role immediately stops every in-flight and future action. Response exercises show continued activity shortly after removal. How should the containment design change?","answers":{"A":"Use a rehearsed combination of runtime or traffic-path isolation and credential/access revocation, then verify that calls stop while allowing for IAM propagation.","B":"Keep role removal as the only control and classify every request after the change as impossible.","C":"Remove the agent from a nested Google group instead, because nested membership changes are always instantaneous.","D":"Wait for the agent to acknowledge a stop instruction before taking any infrastructure action."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"IAM access changes are eventually consistent and can take minutes or longer to propagate. Containment should not depend on an instantaneous revocation guarantee. Rehearsed runtime and traffic controls plus verification address continuing activity; nested-group removal can be slower, and model cooperation is not a reliable control. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/access-change-propagation"},{"id":"question-483","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A production agent on GKE loads a third-party MCP adapter from a mutable latest image tag. Reviewers cannot link the running adapter to its tested source or build. Which release control most directly addresses this supply-chain gap?","answers":{"A":"Keep the mutable tag and rely on Model Armor to determine whether the container was built from approved source.","B":"Deploy reviewed immutable digests with verified provenance, attestations, and an enforced Binary Authorization policy.","C":"Register the adapter's name in Agent Registry and treat registry membership as proof of build integrity.","D":"Restrict the adapter's prompt length so that unreviewed code has less opportunity to run."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Provenance and attestations connect an immutable artifact to required build and review processes, and Binary Authorization can enforce deployment policy on GKE. These controls do not prove all code is harmless, but directly address artifact trust. Content screening, registry metadata, and prompt length do not verify build integrity. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/overview"},{"id":"question-484","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A team has embedded hundreds of frequently changing product FAQs in its Agent Designer instructions. It wants maintainable behavioral guidance and knowledge that follows the source's updates. Which structure best meets these requirements?","answers":{"A":"Use a separate subagent for each FAQ, with the answer stored as that subagent's system instruction.","B":"Keep behavior in instructions and retrieve product knowledge from a connected, regularly synchronized data store.","C":"Keep all FAQs in instructions and create a second instruction section that lists the dates of recent source changes.","D":"Replace the FAQs with model-generated summaries in the instructions and regenerate those summaries during each user session."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Instructions specify conduct; a connected data store supplies maintained enterprise knowledge through retrieval. FAQ-specific agents and duplicated instruction sections multiply maintenance. Per-session summaries do not establish a reliable connection to source updates. Synchronization must be configured for the chosen connector. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/workflow-builder/create-chat-agent; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/connect-cloud-storage"},{"id":"question-485","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A generative CX Agent Studio agent must retrieve an order's current status from an existing HTTPS REST endpoint during a conversation. The endpoint already enforces order access and accepts a supported authentication method. Which low-code integration should the team configure?","answers":{"A":"An OpenAPI tool describing the endpoint, its inputs, and authentication, attached to the agent.","B":"A data store containing a daily export of orders, with instructions to label its results as current.","C":"A static variable holding the endpoint URL, with a prompt telling the model to infer the HTTP request schema.","D":"A Dialogflow CX webhook resource in an unrelated agent, with its resource name added to the Studio agent's description."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An attached OpenAPI tool exposes the existing REST operation for runtime invocation. A daily export can be stale, a URL does not define an executable tool, and an unrelated Dialogflow webhook is not automatically available to a Studio agent. Endpoint authorization remains necessary. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/tool/open-api"},{"id":"question-486","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Dialogflow CX form-filling flow must retain its explicit state transitions, but callers sometimes ask the agent to repeat itself or pause. These inputs reach a no-match handler. The team wants context-sensitive recovery language and a prescribed response if generation fails. What should it configure?","answers":{"A":"An unconditional transition to the start page for every unmatched utterance, resetting the form's progress.","B":"Generative fallback on the relevant no-match handlers, with a recovery prompt and a static fallback response.","C":"A generative prompt that rewrites the transition routes whenever it detects a question outside the current form.","D":"A separate search data store for unmatched utterances, relying on indexing to determine the next page."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Generative fallback can produce a recovery response on a no-match event; the prescribed response is used when generation is unsuccessful. Existing flow routing remains explicitly configured. Resetting the form loses progress, and neither runtime route rewriting nor indexing utterances implements this recovery mechanism. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/generative-fallback"},{"id":"question-487","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An assistant uses Agent Search's answer method over approved equipment manuals. Its custom chat interface currently displays only answerText. Reviewers require users to see which retrieved material supports a generated answer. Which change addresses the missing source references?","answers":{"A":"Store the manual URL in the system prompt and append it to every answer, including answers supported by other manuals.","B":"Enable answer citations and render the returned citation-to-reference mappings in the chat interface.","C":"Increase retrieval top-k and display only the highest-ranked document title next to the entire conversation.","D":"Enable citation generation in the request and continue rendering only answerText to keep the interface unchanged."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Citations identify support for answer spans through returned references, so the client must display those mappings. Merely requesting citations does not render them. A fixed URL or the first search result can misattribute support. Citations make evidence inspectable; they do not guarantee the answer is correct. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/answer"},{"id":"question-488","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A bank is adding generative product Q&A to a CX Agent Studio application. It already has a validated Dialogflow CX account-closure flow that collects required information and calls an authorization-enforcing backend. The closure sequence must remain explicitly controlled. Which design fits?","answers":{"A":"Give the generative Q&A agent the closure endpoint and include the existing flow transcript as a few-shot example.","B":"Retain closure in a flow-based agent and use the generative agent for product Q&A.","C":"Move closure steps into the shared system instructions and reduce temperature so the model follows the same sequence.","D":"Use a single FAQ data store for product answers and account-closure steps, returning the closest matching procedure."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Reusing the validated flow preserves explicit procedural control while the generative agent handles informational conversation. Prompt examples and sampling changes do not create a deterministic workflow, and retrieving a procedure does not execute its required states. Backend authorization remains part of the retained design. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/flow"},{"id":"question-489","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A low-code knowledge assistant retrieves text produced by a Gemini-based preprocessing workflow for safety-training videos. Pilot transcripts accurately capture narration but omit visually demonstrated equipment settings. Which ingestion change best addresses this gap?","answers":{"A":"Increase overlap between transcript chunks so each retrieved passage contains more narration from neighboring segments.","B":"Process the visual content as well as audio and index time-referenced descriptions linked to the original video.","C":"Use a larger text embedding model while keeping exactly the same transcript text as the source material.","D":"Add video duration and resolution to the existing transcript metadata, then rank high-resolution recordings first."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Video understanding can extract information from visible actions and settings that an audio transcript omits. Time references connect descriptions to the original evidence. Overlap, text embeddings, and technical file metadata cannot recover unprocessed visual content. This is an explicit preprocessing design, not automatic raw-video ingestion by every data store. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/capabilities/video-understanding"},{"id":"question-490","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A team wants an Agent Search application over its public documentation website, a BigQuery table of product records, and PDF manuals in Cloud Storage. It needs searchable record fields as well as document content. Which ingestion plan best preserves these sources' useful structure?","answers":{"A":"Export the BigQuery table to PDF and merge it with the manuals, using the website URL as a metadata field.","B":"Use website indexing, structured BigQuery ingestion with a suitable schema, and unstructured document ingestion for the PDFs.","C":"Import URLs for all three sources into a structured table and rely on the query model to download their contents.","D":"Load all source content into a single free-text field so the same parser and retrieval settings apply everywhere."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Source-appropriate ingestion retains structured fields and searchable document content. URLs alone do not ingest the underlying records or files. Flattening every source into free text or PDFs discards useful structure. Configure the app's supported combination of data stores and the appropriate access settings. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/prepare-data; https://www.skills.google/focuses/71943?parent=catalog"},{"id":"question-491","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An Agent Search application contains current and superseded policy documents, each tagged with policy_status. Audit users need the historical documents, but the employee assistant must retrieve only current policies. Which control most directly implements the employee requirement?","answers":{"A":"Apply a large positive boost to current policies and keep superseded policies eligible for every query.","B":"Apply a metadata filter that restricts the employee assistant's searches to policy_status=current.","C":"Set a recent publication-date preference and ask the model to decide whether older documents remain valid.","D":"Delete superseded documents from the shared data store and tell audit users to use the same assistant."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A metadata filter restricts the eligible result set while leaving historical documents available to other searches. Boosting and date preferences alter ranking rather than excluding superseded material. Deletion conflicts with the audit requirement. Ensure the metadata field and query filter are configured as supported. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/filter-search-metadata"},{"id":"question-492","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A team is considering a chain-of-thought-style in-console prompt for a low-code policy assistant. It wants the agent to check eligibility criteria before proposing an answer. Which statement correctly describes how the architect should use this technique?","answers":{"A":"Treat a request for step-by-step reasoning as a guarantee that every hidden reasoning step is exposed and faithfully logged.","B":"Specify the relevant task steps and desired answer, compare quality on representative cases, and avoid treating generated reasoning as a deterministic audit trace.","C":"Require a long explanation after every answer and infer that longer explanations demonstrate more reliable policy compliance.","D":"Replace backend eligibility validation with reasoning instructions once the assistant produces convincing justifications in the console."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Reasoning prompts can help some tasks, but their effect should be measured. They do not establish deterministic execution or a faithful record of all internal computation. A generated justification does not replace validation. Google also advises testing whether a thinking model benefits from explicit reasoning instructions. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/prompts/explain-reasoning; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/prompts/prompt-design-strategies"},{"id":"question-493","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Dialogflow CX booking flow reused in CX Agent Studio must collect destination, travel date, and passenger count before entering a confirmation page. Which configuration avoids relying on the model to decide whether collection is complete?","answers":{"A":"Ask for all three values in the page entry response and transition when any booking intent matches.","B":"Define the values as required form parameters and transition when $page.params.status = \"FINAL\".","C":"Define optional parameters with defaults and transition after the user sends three messages.","D":"Create a no-match handler that moves to confirmation whenever the last response contains a number."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Required form parameters provide explicit collection state, and FINAL indicates that the page's form is complete. An intent match, message count, or numeric response does not establish that all required values exist. Optional defaults can conceal missing user input instead of collecting it. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/parameter"},{"id":"question-494","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A CX Agent Studio order-support agent uses a tool to retrieve an account balance that may change during the conversation. Subsequent instructions need to reference the newest returned value. Which configuration fits this changing state?","answers":{"A":"Put the first returned balance in a static variable compiled into the instructions for the remainder of the session.","B":"Have the tool update a dynamic variable and reference that variable in the agent's instructions.","C":"Store the balance as the default value of the agent's global configuration and reuse it for subsequent users.","D":"Include balance examples in the instructions and ask the model to select the example closest to the tool response."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Dynamic variables are intended for values that tools or other runtime mechanisms update during a session. Static prompt substitutions suit infrequently changing context; examples and shared defaults do not represent the current account result. Authorization must still be enforced by the balance lookup. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/variable"},{"id":"question-495","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"Marketing supplies a finite list of competitor names and requires a CX Agent Studio assistant to replace responses containing any listed name with an approved message. Which no-code guardrail most directly implements this literal output restriction?","answers":{"A":"Enable the strict safety preset and rely on harmful-content categories to identify competitor names.","B":"Configure a blocklist for agent responses with the appropriate matching mode and a 'Say exactly' outcome.","C":"Add the competitor names as few-shot examples and instruct the model to omit them from future answers.","D":"Apply the list only to user input and assume every generated response is covered by that same check."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A response blocklist checks specified words or phrases and can substitute an approved response. Safety categories do not encode this business-specific list. Prompt instructions are weaker than an explicit output check, and input-only filtering does not inspect generated text. This tests literal matching, not a guarantee against all semantic discussion of competitors. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/guardrail"},{"id":"question-496","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A contact center integrates CX Agent Studio and Agent Assist through the bidirectional API. On escalation, the human should continue the same conversation with Agent Assist. Which handoff configuration supports that requirement?","answers":{"A":"Set liveAgentHandoff.endSession to true and reconnect the same CX Agent Studio session after the human joins.","B":"Use the end_session tool for escalation and keep liveAgentHandoff.endSession false.","C":"Return an escalation message to the customer without invoking a handoff, then wait for Agent Assist to detect the text.","D":"Create a new Agent Assist conversation and end the existing one, relying on matching phone numbers to merge their histories."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For this integration, the handoff leaves the conversation open so Agent Assist can create the human participant and continue it. Ending the session prevents this continuation; an escalation message alone does not transfer control. A new conversation is not automatically merged by matching a phone number. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/agent-assist/handoff-cxas"},{"id":"question-497","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Gemini Enterprise third-party connector is configured, but its first ingestion fails with a source API authorization error before any records are read. The integration credentials were created without the connector's required source-system scopes. What should the architect change first?","answers":{"A":"Grant the connector credentials the documented source permissions and retry ingestion.","B":"Grant all end users Editor access to the Google Cloud project so the source API accepts the connector.","C":"Disable data-store ACL enforcement temporarily so it stops filtering documents during the initial API request.","D":"Increase the connector's synchronization frequency so another run can obtain the permissions left out of the first attempt."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Source API authentication and scopes govern whether the connector can read the source. Grant only the permissions documented for that connector. Project roles for end users, disabling retrieval ACLs, or more frequent retries do not correct the source authorization failure. The credential type depends on the connector. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/connect-third-party-data-source; https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/introduction-to-connectors-and-data-stores"},{"id":"question-498","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A CX Agent Studio application has a parent triage agent and a child account-service agent. A trusted authentication tool sets the boolean variable authenticated. Without writing a callback, the architect wants to block handoff to the child until authenticated is true. Which configuration meets this need?","answers":{"A":"Put a prominent authentication reminder in both agents' instructions and let the parent decide when it has been satisfied.","B":"Add a variable-based handoff rule that blocks transfer to the child until authenticated is true.","C":"Add a few-shot conversation showing successful authentication and use it as the routing policy.","D":"Make the child agent's description mention authentication and rely on description matching to prevent premature transfers."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Handoff rules can deterministically gate parent-child transfers using variables, including booleans. Instructions, examples, and descriptions guide the model but do not enforce the transfer condition. The authentication variable must come from a trusted mechanism, and the account service must retain its own access checks. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/cx-agent-studio/handoff"},{"id":"question-499","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Dialogflow CX data-store agent has 500 approved FAQ pairs that should be returned without rewording when a user question matches with high confidence. It also has 300 long PDF manuals for broader questions. Which ingestion design matches these different requirements?","answers":{"A":"Import both sources as unstructured documents and depend on a low temperature to preserve the exact FAQ wording.","B":"Import the pairs into an FAQ data store using the required CSV schema and the PDFs into an unstructured data store.","C":"Import the CSV as unstructured content and use its title column to prevent generative rewriting of matched answers.","D":"Convert the PDFs to question-answer pairs automatically and put everything in an FAQ store without reviewing the generated answers."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Dialogflow CX FAQ stores can return the supplied answer unchanged for a high-confidence question match. Their CSV schema requires question and answer fields; unstructured stores support longer manuals. Unstructured CSV may yield rewritten answers, and automatic conversion does not establish approved FAQ wording. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/data-store"},{"id":"question-500","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Dialogflow CX flow calls an order-status webhook from a route that has no transition target. A timeout currently leaves callers without a useful response. The team needs a dedicated recovery page for timeouts while retaining separate handling for other backend errors. Which configuration should it add?","answers":{"A":"A webhook.error.timeout event handler in the applicable page or flow scope, targeting the recovery page.","B":"A sys.no-input handler that targets the recovery page when the caller pauses after the webhook request.","C":"A catch-all webhook.error handler as the sole handler for every failure, regardless of error type.","D":"A confirmation-page transition on the original webhook route, with timeout instructions in that page's description."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The timeout-specific webhook event supports a dedicated recovery path. No-input describes caller input, and a single generic error handler does not distinguish timeouts. Transitioning to confirmation on the original route is not error recovery and can prevent webhook error events from being invoked. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/dialogflow/cx/docs/concept/handler"},{"id":"question-501","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A nightly job imports a complete Cloud Storage snapshot into a dedicated Gemini Enterprise data store using INCREMENTAL reconciliation. New and updated documents appear, but documents removed from the snapshot remain searchable. The index must mirror the snapshot after each successful import. What should change?","answers":{"A":"Increase the incremental import frequency so deleted documents are eventually recognized as missing.","B":"Keep incremental reconciliation and assign new document IDs each night to replace the previous import.","C":"Use FULL reconciliation for the complete snapshot.","D":"Enable a recency boost so documents absent from the snapshot are ranked below newly imported documents."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"FULL reconciliation rebases the data store against the supplied snapshot and removes absent documents. INCREMENTAL performs upserts; it does not infer deletions from absence. New IDs create distinct records, and ranking changes do not remove obsolete data. The snapshot must be complete for the intended data-store scope. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/connect-cloud-storage"},{"id":"question-502","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"An enterprise source's connector supports both federation and ingestion into Gemini Enterprise. A business constraint prohibits copying the source corpus into the Gemini Enterprise index, and the team accepts the documented search-quality tradeoff. Which connection mode fits?","answers":{"A":"Use ingestion with frequent incremental synchronization to minimize the time the indexed copy differs from the source.","B":"Use federation.","C":"Use ingestion with a short retention policy so only a temporary copy of the source corpus remains indexed.","D":"Use ingestion protected by a customer-managed encryption key to keep the copied corpus outside the indexing requirement."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Federation retrieves information from the source without copying it into the Gemini Enterprise index. Faster synchronization, short retention, and encryption still involve an indexed copy. This choice depends on the selected connector supporting federation and on accepting its search-quality and source-dependency tradeoffs. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/introduction-to-connectors-and-data-stores"},{"id":"question-503","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A Gemini-based preprocessing step analyzes demonstration videos for a low-code knowledge assistant. It samples one frame per second and misses brief indicator flashes between sampled frames. The selected model supports configurable video frame sampling. Which change most directly addresses the missing evidence?","answers":{"A":"Increase the text context window while preserving the existing sampled video frames.","B":"Increase overlap between transcript chunks to preserve more neighboring narration.","C":"Request a more detailed final summary from the same one-frame-per-second representation.","D":"Increase the video sampling rate and validate detection on representative clips."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Events between sampled frames may never reach the model. A higher sampling rate can retain the temporal evidence, subject to supported model limits and cost. More text context, overlapping narration, or a longer summary cannot recover visual frames that were not supplied. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/capabilities/video-understanding"},{"id":"question-504","domain":"1 - Low-code tools","question":"A team is creating a chat agent with the low-code Agent Designer experience in Gemini Enterprise. The required Cloud Storage data store is already connected to the app, but a conversational creation prompt does not attach this Cloud source. What is the documented next step?","answers":{"A":"Repeat the creation prompt with the data store's resource name and an instruction to ignore source-category restrictions.","B":"Upload a sample file from the bucket and assume that doing so connects the complete data store.","C":"Switch to designer mode and select the connected data source in the agent's data and tools configuration.","D":"Grant the agent creator project Owner access and leave the agent's data-source configuration unchanged."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Conversational creation prompts cannot connect Cloud sources such as Cloud Storage or BigQuery. Designer mode provides the data-source configuration. Uploading one file is not a connection to the complete store, and broader IAM access does not replace attaching the required source. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/workflow-builder/create-chat-agent"},{"id":"question-505","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"On a supported Antigravity workstation, a team selected a security preset and assumed all terminal commands were sandboxed. An audit finds sandbox mode disabled and broad unsandboxed command grants. Which correction meets the intended boundary?","answers":{"A":"Keep the preset and add an Always On rule requiring every shell command to mention that it is running in a sandbox.","B":"Enable sandbox mode explicitly, review filesystem and network grants, and remove or narrowly control unsandboxed exceptions.","C":"Change terminal auto-execution to Always Proceed, because automatic execution activates the strongest available isolation mode.","D":"Retain the unsandboxed grants and restrict only browser domains, because terminal commands inherit browser isolation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Presets do not enable Antigravity's sandbox. Sandboxing needs explicit activation and appropriately scoped access; unsandboxed exceptions execute on the host. Prose rules, auto-execution settings alone, and browser controls do not establish the intended terminal boundary. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/sandbox/"},{"id":"question-506","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A monorepo contains backend, mobile, and Terraform code. Detailed Terraform style guidance should enter the Antigravity agent's context when it works on infrastructure files, without requiring developers to remember manual activation. Which rule mode best fits?","answers":{"A":"An Always On rule holding every team's language-specific standards so the model can select the applicable section itself.","B":"A Glob rule scoped to the infrastructure file patterns, with enforcement checks retained in the Terraform pipeline.","C":"A Manual rule linked from the infrastructure README, relying on the agent to infer that it should be activated.","D":"A global rule on each developer's machine, changed manually whenever they switch from application to infrastructure tasks."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Glob activation applies guidance to matching files. Always On unnecessarily broadens context, Manual requires activation, and per-machine global switching is fragile. Rule activation supplies instructions; it does not replace validation or authorization controls. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/rules-workflows/"},{"id":"question-507","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"A team repeatedly delegates read-only architecture reviews to an Antigravity subagent. It wants a repository-maintained reviewer role with a discoverable purpose and restricted tool surface. What should it create?","answers":{"A":"A transient reviewer prompt in a single conversation and rely on all future sessions to inherit that conversation history.","B":"A workspace custom-subagent Markdown definition with metadata, review instructions, and only the necessary inspection tools.","C":"A skill containing the phrase 'be a reviewer' and assume activating it automatically starts a separate concurrent session.","D":"A plugin manifest naming the reviewer, without supplying any agent definition, instructions, or supported tools."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Custom subagent definitions package reusable roles and allowed tools for delegation. Transient prompts are session-specific, skill activation does not itself create a subagent, and a manifest name alone does not define behavior. Inherited permission boundaries still apply. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/subagents/"},{"id":"question-508","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An administrator runs agents-cli update on a developer workstation and reports that a new payments skill is now rolled out to every deployed production agent. What is the appropriate architectural correction?","answers":{"A":"The update succeeds only if every production agent is restarted; after restart, the developer workstation's global skills become their runtime configuration.","B":"The command refreshes skills for detected coding assistants in its installation scope; deployed-agent changes require their own versioned rollout and verification.","C":"The command updates only model weights, so each production agent must also run an evaluation before it can see the revised skill.","D":"The command inventories production agents automatically but applies skill updates only to entries already registered in Agent Registry."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Agents CLI update refreshes installed development skills, with a workspace-scope option. It does not document a production skill inventory or fleet rollout. Runtime deployment and adoption evidence are separate concerns; model weights are not the command's target. Reference: https://google.github.io/agents-cli/cli/#update"},{"id":"question-509","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity MCP server exposes read_ticket and update_ticket. A policy allows mcp(tickets/read_ticket) but also places mcp(tickets/*) in Ask. Reads still prompt. The team wants unattended reads and approval for updates. What should change?","answers":{"A":"Move the read_ticket Allow entry to the top of the file so it is evaluated before the broader Ask entry.","B":"Narrow Ask to update_ticket while retaining the specific Allow for read_ticket and the required backend authorization.","C":"Add another read_ticket Allow entry at workspace scope so two matching allowances outvote the broad Ask rule.","D":"Replace the server policy with mcp(*) in Allow and rely on a workspace rule to ask before modifying a ticket."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Antigravity evaluates Deny before Ask before Allow; a broad Ask match overrides a narrower Allow. Narrowing the Ask target removes the conflict. File order, duplicate allowances, and prose instructions do not implement the requested permission split. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/permissions/"},{"id":"question-510","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An Antigravity agent may read an internal staging website, but submitting forms must require separate authorization. The architect has granted read_url for the site's domain. Which additional decision is correct?","answers":{"A":"Treat the read_url grant as authorization for all clicks and form submissions on that domain, because navigation and actuation share one permission.","B":"Keep execute_url approval-controlled for the domain; reading a page and actuating its interface have separate permissions.","C":"Put the same domain in a workspace coding rule and assume the rule automatically turns all form submissions into approval requests.","D":"Remove the read_url grant and allow unrestricted terminal curl commands so the agent can submit forms without using the browser."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"read_url permits loading and viewing; execute_url governs interactive browser actions. The permission types are distinct. A prose rule is not that policy, and unrestricted shell access could create another route for the same state-changing request. Reference: https://antigravity.google/docs/permissions/"},{"id":"question-511","domain":"2 - Coding agents","question":"An agent's integration-test harness requires a privileged container and direct access to a host Docker socket. The platform plans to run untrusted repository code in GKE Sandbox. What is the best architecture decision?","answers":{"A":"Run the test Pod with privileged mode and a hostPath Docker-socket mount while retaining the gVisor runtime class.","B":"Redesign the harness for non-privileged execution or move the incompatible step to a separately isolated, tightly scoped runner.","C":"Disable gVisor for the entire coding-agent fleet and treat Kubernetes namespaces as an equivalent host-kernel isolation boundary.","D":"Grant the Pod a broader Google Cloud IAM role, because IAM permissions enable privileged Linux system calls inside gVisor."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"GKE Sandbox does not support privileged containers or hostPath storage and limits host-kernel access. The incompatible step needs redesign or separate containment. Namespace separation is not equivalent, and cloud IAM does not remove runtime restrictions. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/sandbox-pods"},{"id":"question-512","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An ADK chat application runs on three replicas. Two requests for the same conversation can load the same persisted state, and their overlapping turns produce inconsistent order details. The product requires turns within one conversation to execute in order, while different conversations remain concurrent. Which coordination boundary fits?","answers":{"A":"One global lock for the entire agent fleet, serializing every user's conversation.","B":"An in-process lock on each replica, with no coordination between replicas.","C":"Cross-replica serialization keyed by the conversation identity, using fresh state for each turn.","D":"Parallel execution of every turn, accepting whichever full-session snapshot is written last."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Durable session storage alone does not establish the application's required turn ordering. Serialize the same conversation across replicas while allowing separate conversations to proceed independently. A local lock does not cover other replicas, a global lock over-serializes unrelated work, and last-writer-wins can lose dependent updates. Reference: https://adk.dev/sessions/session/ ; https://adk.dev/sessions/state/"},{"id":"question-513","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"Choose two. A search team uses a supported Gemini embedding model with retrieval task types. It currently embeds both documents and natural-language search queries with SEMANTIC_SIMILARITY. Which two configurations align the inputs with their retrieval roles?","answers":{"A":"Use RETRIEVAL_DOCUMENT for corpus passages.","B":"Use CLASSIFICATION for corpus passages because each passage will receive a relevance label.","C":"Use RETRIEVAL_QUERY for search queries.","D":"Use RETRIEVAL_DOCUMENT for queries as well, because all texts must use identical task labels."},"correct_answer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Retrieval task types distinguish stored evidence from queries within a compatible embedding setup. Matching the model does not require identical role labels. SEMANTIC_SIMILARITY targets similarity assessment, and CLASSIFICATION targets another task; neither substitutes for the documented query/document retrieval pairing. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/embeddings/task-types"},{"id":"question-514","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A document is withdrawn from a custom RAG corpus, but its chunks and cached answers remain available until the next index rebuild. The application must stop serving that document as soon as withdrawal is recorded. Which lifecycle design fits?","answers":{"A":"Raise the similarity threshold so the withdrawn chunks are less likely to appear.","B":"Delete the source PDF only and wait for vector similarity to stop matching its old chunks.","C":"Ask the model to avoid withdrawn documents while continuing to provide their cached text.","D":"Enforce document eligibility before context assembly, invalidate affected caches, and remove all indexed chunks through the update pipeline."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Index refresh can lag source lifecycle changes. An enforced eligibility check can block withdrawn content while chunk deletion propagates; associated caches must respect the same state. Similarity thresholds or prompt requests are not withdrawal controls, and deleting a source file does not by itself delete copied vectors and content. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/vector-search/update-rebuild-index ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/data-source-access-control"},{"id":"question-515","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A supported Google Cloud MCP server exposes many tools, but an agent only needs one documented read-oriented toolset. Loading every tool increases context cost and selection mistakes. What is the most targeted integration change?","answers":{"A":"Configure the documented toolset endpoint and retain the required IAM restrictions.","B":"Load every tool but lower generation temperature to make unwanted tools inaccessible.","C":"Replace IAM with the toolset name, treating selection of an endpoint as a complete permission grant.","D":"Keep the full tool catalog and add a prompt-level allowlist limiting the tools the agent should select."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A supported toolset narrows exposed capabilities and context. IAM still controls access. Temperature and prompt allowlists do not remove loaded definitions or enforce resource permissions. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/mcp/overview"},{"id":"question-516","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"An A2A partner returns a task in input-required state because it needs a date range. The client has now obtained that range. How should it continue the work?","answers":{"A":"Treat input-required as a terminal failure and create an unrelated task with no prior context.","B":"Send the missing information as a follow-up associated with the existing task and context.","C":"Create a new task containing only the date range and assume the partner automatically merges it into the interrupted task.","D":"Keep polling without supplying input, expecting the task to infer the range after a timeout."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"input-required is an interrupted task awaiting information, not a completed or failed task. A follow-up can continue that task. Polling does not supply missing input, and a new task is not automatically merged with the interrupted one. Terminal tasks instead require a new task for further work. Reference: https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/life-of-a-task/"},{"id":"question-517","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A Firestore transaction used by an agent updates a job record and directly calls a SaaS API to send a notification. Under contention, the transaction function reruns and the user receives duplicate notifications. Which design handles the retry boundary?","answers":{"A":"Raise the transaction retry limit and catch notification errors inside the transaction function.","B":"Move the API call before the transaction and omit any durable record tying it to the job.","C":"Commit a notification-intent record with the job update, then deliver it through an idempotent dispatcher.","D":"Keep the API call in the transaction and rely on database rollback to undo the remote notification."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Firestore may rerun transaction functions; rollback cannot undo an independent SaaS action. Persisting intent atomically and dispatching it with operation deduplication handles that boundary. More retries amplify duplication, and a detached pre-transaction send can occur even when the job update never commits. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/firestore/native/docs/manage-data/transactions ; https://google.aip.dev/155"},{"id":"question-518","domain":"3 - Developing custom agents","question":"A research agent must compare the retention policy of Product A with Product B and explain the difference. One broad query retrieves many Product A passages but little evidence for Product B. Both sets of documents are in the authorized corpus. Which retrieval plan best addresses the comparison?","answers":{"A":"Increase answer length so the generator can infer the missing Product B policy.","B":"Rerank the existing Product A candidates and treat the highest score as support for both products.","C":"Reuse an earlier generated product comparison without checking its underlying Product B evidence.","D":"Retrieve evidence for each product through focused subqueries, then synthesize a comparison supported on both sides."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A comparison needs evidence for both entities. Focused subqueries can address coverage missing from the broad search, followed by evidence-based synthesis. Reordering one-sided candidates, extending generation, or reusing an unsupported prior answer does not establish the other product's facts. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/answer"},{"id":"question-525","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A release report says the candidate passed evaluation. Its recorded configuration is model=v2, temperature=0.2, prompt=p17. The deployment manifest specifies model=v1, temperature=0.8, prompt=p17. What is the strongest release conclusion?","answers":{"A":"The shared prompt version is sufficient evidence because model settings do not change tool selection.","B":"Run the same evaluator against the intended deployment configuration before using the results as its release evidence.","C":"Promote the candidate if the evaluation model is newer, since that makes the benchmark conservative.","D":"Use the previous production latency report to correct the quality score for the different temperatures."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Evaluation results describe a particular system configuration. Different model versions or generation settings can change behavior, so the evidence must match what will be deployed. A common prompt, presumed model ordering, or a latency adjustment cannot establish quality for an unevaluated configuration. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluation-overview"},{"id":"question-526","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A ticket-routing agent must emit valid JSON whose priority is one of three allowed values and whose ticket_id exactly matches a supplied record. These rules are fully deterministic. Which evaluator is most appropriate for this requirement?","answers":{"A":"A custom LLM rubric that estimates whether the output appears to satisfy the schema and record identity.","B":"A calibrated pairwise judge comparing the response to a fluent narrative reference about the ticket.","C":"A custom code metric validating the schema, allowed values, and identifier equality.","D":"A response-similarity metric that passes when most field names overlap with a valid example."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Custom code metrics directly test deterministic constraints. LLM judgments introduce unnecessary uncertainty for exact checks; pairwise preference measures a different objective. Text overlap can remain high despite an invalid enum or a mismatched identifier. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/manage-metrics"},{"id":"question-527","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An ADK eval case contains four invocations. With tool_trajectory_avg_score set to EXACT, three invocations match their expected calls completely. The fourth contains all expected calls plus one extra call. The pass threshold is 1.0. What result should the team expect?","answers":{"A":"A score of 1.0 and a pass, because every expected tool call occurred somewhere in the case.","B":"A partial fourth-invocation score based on its fraction of matching calls, followed by a pass.","C":"A score of 0.0, because one failed invocation forces every other invocation score to zero.","D":"A score of 0.75 and a failure, because each invocation contributes either 1 or 0."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"EXACT assigns zero to the invocation with the extra call and one to each exact match. The case average is 3/4, below 1.0. It does not grant per-call partial credit or automatically zero the entire case for one mismatch. Reference: https://github.com/google/adk-python/blob/main/src/google/adk/evaluation/trajectory_evaluator.py"},{"id":"question-528","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Using Agent Runtime Preview revisions, a team changes a documented unversioned agent setting and observes regressions on both its old and new revisions. It redirects all root-agent traffic to the old revision. What recovery assumption is incorrect?","answers":{"A":"That routing to the old revision restores the previous value of the unversioned setting.","B":"That querying the old active revision directly can help isolate versioned-code behavior.","C":"That the team must separately restore the shared setting if it caused the regression.","D":"That retained artifacts are useful for checking which revision-specific code was deployed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Unversioned-field updates apply across the agent revisions, so a traffic rollback does not restore their earlier values. Direct revision tests and artifact inspection remain useful for versioned behavior. The recovery plan must separately address the changed shared setting. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/manage-revisions-and-traffic"},{"id":"question-529","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A trace shows model planning from 0.0\u20130.4 s. Two independent tools then run concurrently: tool A from 0.4\u20132.4 s and tool B from 0.4\u20133.4 s. Final generation runs from 3.4\u20134.0 s. Which optimization most reduces this request latency, assuming all other times stay unchanged?","answers":{"A":"Reduce tool A from 2.0 to 1.0 s; the request then completes at 3.0 s.","B":"Reduce tool B from 3.0 to 2.0 s; the request then completes at 3.0 s.","C":"Reduce both tool durations by 0.2 s; their combined savings shorten the request by 0.4 s.","D":"Reduce tool A by 0.5 s; its shorter duration removes 0.5 s from the request critical path."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The tools overlap, so their contribution is the longer duration, not their sum. Reducing the three-second tool to two seconds removes one second from the critical path. Speeding only the already-shorter tool does not change completion time; equal 0.2-second reductions save 0.2 seconds. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/scale/runtime/tracing"},{"id":"question-530","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"Experts label 190 of 200 outputs acceptable and 10 as critical failures. An autorater labels every output acceptable and therefore reports 95% agreement. What should the team conclude about using it to detect critical failures?","answers":{"A":"Its high agreement makes it suitable; the ten disagreements can be absorbed into the normal release error budget.","B":"Its error is only 5%, so a larger production traffic sample will eliminate the false-negative problem.","C":"It misses every critical failure; evaluate per-class detection and recalibrate before relying on this gate.","D":"It needs a more balanced score scale; converting binary labels to ratings from one to five resolves the disagreement."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Aggregate agreement conceals class imbalance. This judge has zero recall for the critical-failure class and would let every labeled dangerous case through. More traffic or a different numerical scale does not correct that behavior; confusion-matrix and per-class analysis expose it. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/evaluate-judge-model"},{"id":"question-531","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"An online evaluation monitor receives timing spans but cannot assess whether responses followed tool instructions. The exported events omit model inputs, outputs, system instructions, and tool definitions. What should the team change first?","answers":{"A":"Lower the evaluation threshold so traces with missing context are less likely to be reported as failures.","B":"Increase the trace sampling rate without changing the exported attributes, creating more timing-only examples.","C":"Replace the online monitor with a final-answer character count, which requires no execution context.","D":"Export the required inference context and agent/session attributes under appropriate data-handling controls."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The monitor needs the semantic telemetry specified by its evaluators, not just timing. More samples cannot recover missing context. Threshold changes and character counts do not assess instruction or tool compliance. Required content capture should follow the organization's access and retention controls. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/optimize/evaluation/evaluate-online"},{"id":"question-532","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A create-order tool times out after sending a request. The agent cannot tell whether the order was committed. The API supports idempotency keys and status lookup by request ID. What is the best recovery policy?","answers":{"A":"Reconcile status and, if a retry is needed, reuse the original idempotency key within a bounded retry policy.","B":"Assume timeout means no order exists and retry with a fresh key to avoid a cached failure response.","C":"Tell the user the order failed and forget the request ID, letting the user submit another order immediately.","D":"Repeat the tool call through two replicas, assigning each retry a different key to maximize completion probability."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A transport timeout leaves the mutation outcome uncertain. Status reconciliation and reuse of the same idempotency key prevent a recovery attempt from creating a second logical order. New keys, parallel mutations, or premature failure claims can cause duplicate or inconsistent outcomes. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/retry-strategy"},{"id":"question-533","domain":"4 - Evaluating and deploying agents","question":"A RAG agent answer score falls after both the retriever and generator change. The team has a reviewed query set, relevance labels, and reference evidence passages. Which experiment best localizes the regression?","answers":{"A":"Compare end-to-end scores for both bundles and attribute any decline to whichever component changed most lines of code.","B":"Evaluate retrieval against labels, then compare generators on fixed evidence while varying one component at a time.","C":"Increase the retrieved chunk count and model size simultaneously, then use any recovery to identify the original cause.","D":"Ask the generator to diagnose whether its retrieval was sufficient and treat its self-assessment as the only metric."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Independent retrieval scores and fixed-context generation tests separate two possible failure sources. Controlled component changes produce interpretable comparisons. End-to-end differences alone, simultaneous modifications, and generator self-diagnosis cannot reliably identify which change caused the decline. Reference: https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/optimizing-rag-retrieval"},{"id":"question-534","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A custom Model Armor integration receives NO_MATCH_FOUND but also invocationResult=PARTIAL because the required sensitive-data filter did not execute. Policy prohibits sending content externally unless every required check succeeds. What should the integration do?","answers":{"A":"Forward the content because the overall match result takes precedence over filter execution failures.","B":"Treat the content as unverified and withhold external transmission until required screening succeeds.","C":"Forward the content after adding an audit entry, because logging compensates for the skipped filter.","D":"Retry the external model request directly, because a second model can perform the missing sensitive-data inspection."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Match status and execution status answer different questions. PARTIAL means some filters failed or were skipped; no match is not evidence that every required check passed. Given the stated policy, transmission must remain blocked pending successful screening. Logging or unscreened retries do not satisfy that policy. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/reference/rest/v1/SanitizationResult"},{"id":"question-535","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A previously approved remote MCP server changes a tool schema to add an upload destination and embeds instructions to send conversation history there. The client receives a tool-list change notification. What is the best governance response?","answers":{"A":"Automatically accept the change because approval of the server applies to every future schema and capability it publishes.","B":"Keep the original tool name on the allowlist and let the model decide whether the new arguments are necessary.","C":"Suspend the changed capability pending review and validate its schema, permissions, and destinations before updating the approved configuration.","D":"Accept the updated tool if its JSON Schema is syntactically valid and it retains its read-only annotation."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"MCP can notify clients that tools changed, but that notification does not establish trust. A capability change can alter data exposure and needs review under the stated approval process. Existing names, schema validity, and self-reported annotations do not prove the changed tool is safe. Reference: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools"},{"id":"question-536","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A new Agent Gateway IAM Access policy denies destructive MCP calls. Staging audit logs show denials, yet the calls still reach the tool. Gateway is configured in DRY_RUN mode. What should the platform team do after confirming the policy matches the intended traffic?","answers":{"A":"Change the gateway to ENFORCE and verify that prohibited calls are blocked.","B":"Keep DRY_RUN and lower the model temperature so the agent stops selecting the destructive tool.","C":"Add another identical deny rule because two dry-run denials cause the gateway to block the request.","D":"Enable Data Access audit logs on the tool project, which changes dry-run authorization into enforced authorization."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Dry-run mode records disallowed communications without blocking them. After validation, ENFORCE applies the policy to traffic. Repeating deny rules, changing model settings, or enabling additional logs does not change the gateway enforcement mode. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/govern/policies/iam-overview-uap"},{"id":"question-537","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A governance administrator copies an agent's inventory identifier beginning urn:agent: from a catalog into an IAM Access policy. The intended agent does not receive the expected authorization. Which identifier belongs in the agent-principal portion of the policy?","answers":{"A":"The agent display name, provided that it is unique within the registry project.","B":"The agent identity's principal:// identifier, derived from its trust domain and hosted resource path.","C":"The same urn:agent: string with the registry location appended to make it globally unique.","D":"The MCP server destination URL, because Agent Gateway infers the calling identity from the destination."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Inventory URNs are for catalog lookup, whereas IAM policies identify an agent using its supported principal identifier. Display names and destination URLs identify different things and cannot replace the authenticated agent principal. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/govern/policies/iam-overview-uap"},{"id":"question-538","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"A team cites Google's restriction on training models with customer data and concludes that its agent has zero data retention. The design also uses optional request-response logging and external grounding features. What must the architect do?","answers":{"A":"Accept the claim because a no-training restriction also prohibits all operational storage and logging.","B":"Disable application logging only; provider feature settings cannot affect retention of prompts or responses.","C":"Confirm that the selected model is generally available, which makes all retention-related settings irrelevant.","D":"Review each selected feature's retention behavior and configure only features and settings compatible with the requirement."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A restriction on training is different from a zero-retention commitment. Logging, grounding, and other features can retain data or require specific settings. The exact model and feature combination must be reviewed; general availability and application logging alone do not determine the full retention behavior. Reference: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/resources/zero-data-retention"},{"id":"question-539","domain":"5 - Security and governance","question":"An agent's output passes content screening, but its web client inserts the text directly into HTML. A retrieved document causes the model to emit executable markup. Which boundary should prevent execution?","answers":{"A":"The system prompt should forbid script tags, and the client should trust output that follows the requested response schema.","B":"The retrieval index should accept only documents that were previously screened, and the client should render their generated summaries directly.","C":"The client should use context-appropriate output encoding and sanitization for any permitted markup.","D":"The model should classify its answer as safe to display, and the client should execute markup only after a positive classification."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Model output is untrusted content at the browser boundary. Correct encoding and sanitization prevent it from being interpreted as executable markup. Prompts, prior retrieval screening, and model self-classification cannot replace safe rendering. Reference: https://adk.dev/safety/"}]};
